# File:  ECAutomatedScan.dgp
# Authors: Tyler Lesthaeghe
# Created: 2026-09-29
# Description:
#   Automated eddy-current inspection: part detection, surface
#   reconstruction, toolpath planning and continuous-motion scanning.
# Derived From:
#
#########################################################################
# Revision Log
#
# 2026-09-29:  Initial Commit
#
#########################################################################
# Development Notes
#
#
#########################################################################
# Automated eddy-current inspection: find the part, measure it, plan over it,
# scan it in continuous motion.
#
# Loading this config SETS EVERYTHING UP AND THEN STOPS. It does not scan on its
# own and it never asks a question. Call the stages from the dgpy> prompt, or
# run_automated_scan() for all of them in order.
#
# THE FIRST RUN ON HARDWARE -- what each stage should print, how it fails, and
# the order to run things in -- is ROS-Scanning/docs/ec-automated-scan-first-run.md.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE OLDER REFERENCE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ClientScannerTest.dgp is the workflow this is built from and is deliberately
# left in place: parts of it are still the only description of how a piece of
# this cell behaves.
#
# Two differences from it matter operationally. clean_mesh() here actually
# filters, where that file's meshFilter() removed nothing, so MESH_CUTOFF_Z has
# to be set for this cell and the output mesh will differ. And the arm does not
# stop at every point to take a reading: this scans in continuous motion, one
# sample per pitch of travel, correlated by timestamp.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT AN EDDY-CURRENT TRACE IS HERE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One complex impedance, not a waveform: the instrument's X and Y outputs on two
# DAQ channels, combined into one sample per trigger. The trace list is (K, 1)
# complex with a pose per entry -- the same shape the ultrasound trace list has
# with nsamples=1, which is what lets the accumulation, projection and toolpath
# code be shared rather than duplicated.
#
# Conditioning -- null, rotation, per-axis gain -- is a compiled math function
# (spatialnde2-ec-functions), because every parameter is a recording so that
# turning the rotation re-renders every trace already acquired.
#
# THE SCAN DISPLAY IS SPATIAL. spatialnde2's phase-plane rendering draws the
# impedance figure but carries no position, so it cannot show where on the part a
# signal came from. Use it to SET the rotation; read the scan off the projected
# horizontal/vertical/magnitude channels.

from dataguzzler_python import dgpy
import spatialnde2 as snde

include(dgpy,"dgpy_startup.dpi")
include(dgpy,"Qt.dpi",prefer_pyqt=False)
include(dgpy,"matplotlib.dpi")
include(dgpy,"pint.dpi")

# ==============================================================================
# TRANSACTION MANAGER -- NOT OPTIONAL FOR THIS CONFIG
# ==============================================================================
# The eddy-current correlation IS the timestamp ordering. Under
# timed_transaction_manager a transaction stamped at time T sees every
# transaction stamped before T already committed, so a pose transaction stamped
# at a trigger crossing produces a globalrev carrying exactly the DAQ block that
# was current then. The ordered manager does not provide that, and it also
# allows only one open transaction at a time -- which wedges an acquisition
# thread publishing while the stager stages.
RECDB_TRANSACTION_MANAGER = "timed"

# The ordering window: a transaction stamped at T is held this long so anything
# stamped earlier can still arrive and commit first. Too small and correlated
# reads see an incomplete picture, reported as late transactions.
#
# 0.02 ON HARDWARE, UNCHANGED AND DELIBERATELY SO. It has run the real cell
# without trouble, the window is latency the correlation pays on every reading,
# and nothing measured here is a reason to widen it there.
#
# WIDER IN SIMULATION, because this machine is not the cell PC. One simulated
# scan reported 33 late transactions since startup, worst 25.4 ms older than its
# predecessor -- committed, but with their globalrev timestamps out of order --
# and the manager refused 22 more at the door. Of those refusals only 2 were the
# simulated instrument (which counts them as dropped samples and carries on);
# the rest belonged to other producers. That is what an eight-core box running
# Gazebo, RViz, MoveIt, the profiler, the reconstruction and the acquisition all
# at once does to thread scheduling, and it is a property of this bench rather
# than of the pipeline.
#
# Overridable so a bench can be told to behave like the cell:
#     TARI_TRANSACTION_LATENCY=0.02 dataguzzler-python ...
#
# Read from the ENVIRONMENT rather than from CELL_MODE, which is not defined
# until further down -- the transaction manager is built immediately below and
# everything else depends on it, so this cannot wait for it. The two are
# cross-checked where CELL_MODE is set, so they cannot drift apart silently.
TRANSACTION_LATENCY = float(
    os.environ.get("TARI_TRANSACTION_LATENCY")
    or (0.02 if (os.environ.get("TARI_CELL_MODE") or "hardware") == "hardware"
        else 0.08))

if RECDB_TRANSACTION_MANAGER != "timed":
    raise RuntimeError(
        "ECAutomatedScan requires RECDB_TRANSACTION_MANAGER='timed'. The "
        "eddy-current acquisition correlates the DAQ with position BY "
        "TIMESTAMP, and the ordered manager commits in thread-completion order "
        "instead -- which produces traces attached to the wrong places rather "
        "than an error.")

transaction_clock = snde.measurement_clock_cpp_steady("")
transmgr = snde.timed_transaction_manager(transaction_clock, TRANSACTION_LATENCY)
include(snde, "recdb.dpi", transmgr=transmgr, enable_opencl=True)
include(snde, "recdb_gui.dpi")

import os
import time
import numpy as np
import rclpy
import copy
import itertools
import threading
from geometry_msgs.msg import TransformStamped, Pose, PoseArray, PoseStamped
from dataguzzler_python import Qt, QtWrapper

rclpy.init()

import dgpython_rosmotion as rosm

global ros
ros = rosm.motion('ros', recdb, 'probe_end', 'world')

# Probe position for anything that runs while the arm is moving. `ros` is a dgpy
# module, so every attribute read takes its context lock, and the thread that
# called ros.Move() holds it until the move returns -- during a pass that is
# seconds with no position available, long enough to bind a whole pass of
# samples to one stale pose. ros.live_pose is a plain threadsafe holder the TF
# thread pushes into. Fetch it once, here, while nothing is moving.
global live_pose
live_pose = ros.live_pose

# ==============================================================================
# WHICH CELL, AND IN WHICH MODE
# ==============================================================================
# The cell manifest under tari_bringup already describes the robot, the sensors
# and the topics; naming it here lets this config read that description instead
# of repeating it. What it reads is the WORK VOLUME -- the box parts are placed
# inside, which every reconstruction and every toolpath planner crops to.
#
# Without it each stage invents its own crop from whatever it happens to know
# and gets it wrong differently each time. modules/work_cell.dpi refuses rather
# than guessing when the manifest declares none.
CELL_NAME = os.environ.get("TARI_CELL_NAME") or "ex-ur10e-1"
# "hardware" unless the environment says otherwise. Taking it from the
# environment is what lets the wiring that only runs at load time -- the cable
# guard, the supervisor, the band handed to the motion module -- be rehearsed
# against the simulated cell without editing this file. The DEFAULT is
# hardware, so a cell PC that sets nothing runs as hardware.
CELL_MODE = os.environ.get("TARI_CELL_MODE") or "hardware"

# The transaction window was chosen from the same environment variable before
# this line existed, because the manager has to be built first. If that ever
# stops being true the window would be the wrong one for the mode and nothing
# would say so, so it is checked rather than assumed.
if not os.environ.get("TARI_TRANSACTION_LATENCY"):
    _want_latency = 0.02 if CELL_MODE == "hardware" else 0.08
    if abs(TRANSACTION_LATENCY - _want_latency) > 1e-12:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "TRANSACTION_LATENCY is %.4f s but CELL_MODE is %r, which wants "
            "%.4f s. The window is chosen from TARI_CELL_MODE before CELL_MODE "
            "exists; they have come apart."
            % (TRANSACTION_LATENCY, CELL_MODE, _want_latency))

# ==============================================================================
# TECHNICIAN CONTROLS
# ==============================================================================
# Set these two. Everything else is derived, clamped or refused by
# modules/scan_parameters.dpi, and every scan prints its plan before it moves.

# Distance between samples along a pass, in metres.
SCAN_STEP_SIZE = 0.001

# How fast the probe sweeps, in metres per second. The sample rate is
# velocity/step, so once the step is chosen this is a rate request: above the
# pipeline's ceiling it is scaled down and reported, far above it raises.
#
# 10 mm/s is a limit, not a preference: the probe is IN CONTACT for the whole
# sweep, and the limit near the part is 10 mm/s (NEAR_PART_SPEED below).
# run_ec_scan() refuses anything faster. It is also exactly the live staging
# ceiling at a 1 mm step (10 traces/s), so nothing gets clamped.
SCAN_VELOCITY = 0.010

# ==============================================================================
# EDDY-CURRENT INSTRUMENT
# ==============================================================================
# The instrument's X and Y outputs, as analog inputs on the DAQ. These become
# recording-database channels with a leading slash.
EC_DAQ_DEVICE = "PXI1Slot2"
EC_CHANNELS = ("ai0", "ai1")
EC_REAL_PATH = "/%s/%s" % (EC_DAQ_DEVICE, EC_CHANNELS[0])
EC_IMAG_PATH = "/%s/%s" % (EC_DAQ_DEVICE, EC_CHANNELS[1])

# Where the eddy-current readings come from.
#
#   "hardware"  the NI-DAQ. The only setting that measures anything.
#   "none"      no instrument at all. Reconstruction and planning still work --
#               which is most of the pipeline and most machines -- and
#               run_ec_scan() refuses rather than inventing data.
#   "sim"       SimECSource (sim_ec_source.dpi): two free-running channels of
#               simulated volts, published exactly the way ai_voltage publishes
#               real ones. What FILLS them depends on EC_TABLE below -- either
#               a real impedance-plane model driven by a generated response
#               table, or, with no table, synthetic bumps that are an
#               ARCHITECTURAL stand-in and not a model of a probe at all. Both
#               exercise the trigger correlation, the trace accumulator, the
#               impedance-plane conditioning and the projection end to end
#               without a card.
#
# "sim" exists so the architecture can be exercised without an instrument. The
# failures it catches are wiring failures: the conditioning must not be attached
# to /ec/traces before the accumulator that creates that channel has run,
# because naming a channel that does not exist yet is SIGABRT for the whole
# process under the timed transaction manager. EC_SOURCE "none" cannot reach
# any of that, because it refuses the scan instead of running it.
#
# Overridable by the EC_SOURCE environment variable so that a simulation run
# does not need this file edited. The cell PC gets ROS-Scanning by hand copy (no
# git yet), so a local edit made to test in simulation travels to the cell on
# the next copy. An environment variable leaves the file that reaches the arm
# saying "hardware".
#
#     EC_SOURCE=sim dataguzzler-python dgpfiles/ECAutomatedScan.dgp
EC_SOURCE = os.environ.get("EC_SOURCE", "hardware")

# Only read when EC_SOURCE == "sim". The block IS what a buffered card returns
# per read, so these two set what EC_BLOCK_REDUCE averages over and what
# report_smear() measures: 1 kHz in blocks of 20 samples, which at the 10 mm/s
# sweep is 0.2 mm of travel per block against a 1.00 mm trace pitch.
EC_SIM_SAMPLE_RATE = 1000.0
EC_SIM_BLOCK_HZ = 50.0

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SIMULATED PROBE RESPONSE -- only read when EC_SOURCE == "sim"
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Point these at a generated response table and a test plate and the simulated
# instrument stops inventing its numbers and starts computing them:
#
#   EC_TABLE  the HDF5 response table. tools/generate_ec_table.py writes it,
#             running against ecsim on a machine with the compute for it (a
#             night on a workstation); the flaw-free lift-off/tilt trajectory
#             sits beside it as *_baseline.npz and is found automatically.
#             tools/make_ec_stub_table.py writes a schema-conformant STUB in
#             seconds for working on the pipeline itself -- its physics is
#             invented and the file says so, and everything that prints a
#             result from it repeats that.
#   EC_PLATE  flaws.json from tools/make_ec_test_plate.py: a flat aluminium
#             plate carrying notches whose dimensions sit exactly on the
#             table's nodes, so no interpolation in flaw geometry is needed and
#             any error seen is the pipeline's. The notches are deliberately
#             NOT in the mesh -- a 0.15 mm slot is below what the profiler
#             resolves and the 4.75 mm probe bridges it -- so the geometry and
#             the flaws are separate, exactly as they are on a real part.
#
# WITH BOTH, the simulated instrument reproduces what we are actually trying to
# fight: a differential probe's double-lobed loop over a notch, and tilt about
# the split line producing a signal of the SAME KIND as a flaw rather than
# noise on top of it. Separating those two is the whole problem, and it cannot
# be rehearsed against synthetic bumps that put them on different axes by
# construction.
#
# WITH NEITHER, the synthetic signal runs and nothing else changes. That is the
# normal case on a machine with no table, and it keeps working with no h5py.
#
#     EC_SOURCE=sim EC_TABLE=~/ectable/al6061_2mhz_response.h5 \
#         EC_PLATE=~/ecplate/flaws.json dataguzzler-python ...
EC_TABLE = os.environ.get("EC_TABLE", "")
EC_PLATE = os.environ.get("EC_PLATE", "")

# The angle from the probe_end frame's x axis to the probe's COIL-SEPARATION
# axis, degrees. NOT KNOWN for the real probe -- it is a pencil probe in a
# holder and its rotation about its own axis was never recorded -- and it
# matters, because it decides which tilt is the dangerous one and rotates every
# notch's apparent orientation by the same amount. Measuring it is on the cell
# visit plan. Zero means the coils lie along probe_end x.
EC_COIL_AXIS_DEG = float(os.environ.get("EC_COIL_AXIS_DEG", "0.0"))

# Instrument output scale, volts per ohm, and None to take the model's own
# default. The real instrument's gain is a front-panel setting and choosing it
# is one of the things this simulation exists to study, so it is a knob here
# too. The model prints what the loaded table suggests for 2 V on its largest
# notch -- which assumes a NULLED instrument, since un-nulled the standing
# reading is two orders of magnitude up on the flaw and that gain rails every
# block.
EC_SIM_V_PER_OHM = (float(os.environ["EC_SIM_V_PER_OHM"])
                    if os.environ.get("EC_SIM_V_PER_OHM") else None)

# What to do with the DAQ block a trigger correlates to. The block spans time and
# the probe moved during it, so this is a SPATIAL averaging choice: "mean" is
# quieter and smears, "last" is closest to the crossing and noisier. The scan
# reports the smear in metres and as a fraction of the step -- check it.
EC_BLOCK_REDUCE = "mean"

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE INSTRUMENT NULL -- BALANCED ON THE PART, NEVER IN AIR
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An eddy-current bridge has to be nulled with the probe ON unflawed material.
# Nulled in air the operating point is in the wrong place, the gain is applied
# about that point, and the whole scan rides at an offset. The scan issues it
# once, at the instant the probe first touches down, before any trace is read.
#
# NOT THE SAME THING AS EC_NULL / /ec/cond/null BELOW. That is a complex number
# subtracted from the recorded traces by the conditioning math, set live with
# null_here() and re-runnable over traces already acquired. This one is a
# hardware operation on the instrument itself, done once, and it cannot be
# re-done after the fact. The software offset sits on top of it and does not
# replace it.
#
# Which instrument:
#   "uniwest"  the Uniwest US-454A on a serial port. UniWest.null() sends its
#              NULL command.
#   "none"     no instrument to null -- bench work, or a simulated source.
EC_INSTRUMENT = os.environ.get("EC_INSTRUMENT",
                               "uniwest" if EC_SOURCE == "hardware" else "none")
# The serial port the instrument answers on, and its baud rate. From
# ClientScannerTest.dgp, where the same two were written by hand.
EC_INSTRUMENT_PORT = int(os.environ.get("EC_INSTRUMENT_PORT", "33"))
EC_INSTRUMENT_BAUD = 9600

# Issue the null at the first touchdown of every scan. False is bench work:
# nothing to balance and nothing being measured. set_ec_nulling() is how to
# change either of these from the prompt.
EC_NULL_ON_CONTACT = True

# What a null that cannot be done means.
#
#   True   run_ec_scan() refuses before it moves when there is no instrument to
#          null, and stops at the first pass if the null command fails. Both
#          happen before any trace is acquired, so nothing measured is thrown
#          away.
#   False  the failure is printed and the scan goes ahead un-nulled.
#
# True whenever there is real hardware to measure with: an un-nulled scan is not
# a scan that needs redoing, it is a scan whose data cannot be recovered.
EC_NULL_REQUIRED = EC_SOURCE == "hardware"

# MEASURE THE LIFT-OFF ROTATION AT THE END OF A SCAN AND RE-PAINT WITH IT.
#
# The projected channel is /ec/cond/vertical, which only means "the part" once
# the rotation lays lift-off along the horizontal axis. Unset, the image is the
# standing offset plus tilt. The angle is a property of this probe, this
# frequency and this instrument today, so it is measured from the scan's own
# traces -- see rotation_from_scan(). Off means the image stays as acquired and
# an operator sets the rotation by hand.
EC_AUTO_ROTATION = True

# Impedance-plane conditioning -- the SOFTWARE offset, rotation and gain, applied
# to recorded traces. NONE OF THESE ARE MEANINGFUL DEFAULTS: the null depends on
# the part and the probe, and the rotation is chosen during setup by turning it
# until lift-off flattens onto the horizontal axis. Identity values give a
# readable but unconditioned display. Use null_here() and set_rotation().
EC_NULL = (0.0, 0.0)
EC_ROTATION_DEG = 0.0
EC_GAIN = (1.0, 1.0)

# Where the trace list and the conditioned channels live.
EC_TRACES_PATH = "/ec/traces"
EC_COND_PREFIX = "/ec/cond"

# ==============================================================================
# TOOLPATH
# ==============================================================================
# Segments shorter than this are link moves between raster lines, not scanning
# passes. Noether reports its own segment boundaries, so this only has to
# separate "line" from "hop between lines" -- and at a 0.3 mm line spacing a
# hop is 0.3 mm. 5 mm rather than the ultrasound scan's 20 mm: real-cell
# eddy-current regions are 10-30 mm across, and at 20 mm every pass across such
# a region would be thrown away as a link move.
PATH_MIN_SEGMENT_M = 0.005

# How much of a pass has to be reachable for it to count. A surface-following
# toolpath is ragged and some passes will not plan in full; with a trace list
# every sample carries the pose it was taken at, so a partial pass costs
# coverage there and nothing anywhere else.
PATH_MIN_FRACTION = 0.6

# ==============================================================================
# MESH FILTERING
# ==============================================================================
# A plane in WORLD z, below which vertices are discarded. There is no sensible
# default: it is a property of where this cell's bench is. On the UR10e cell the
# table reconstructs at z 0.938-0.956 and a specimen sits from 0.998 up, so
# anything in 0.96-0.99 separates them. None means DO NOT run the vertex filter,
# which is what lets loose-part removal run instead -- the two are mutually
# exclusive in the service.
MESH_CUTOFF_Z = None

def _scan_module_dir():
    """Locate dgpfiles/modules regardless of where this config was launched from.

    include() resolves relative paths against the launcher's working directory,
    not against this file, so 'modules/foo.dpi' only works from one place."""
    here = os.path.abspath(os.getcwd())
    for _ in range(4):
        for cand in (os.path.join(here, "dgpfiles", "modules"),
                     os.path.join(here, "modules")):
            if os.path.isdir(cand):
                return cand
        here = os.path.dirname(here)
    raise RuntimeError("cannot find dgpfiles/modules from %s" % (os.getcwd(),))

# ==============================================================================
# THE REST OF THE SETTINGS
# ==============================================================================
# Values carried over from UTPlaneScan.dgp, which is where they were measured.
# Only the ones eddy current genuinely changes are different, and those say so.

# --- motion -------------------------------------------------------------------
MOVE_VELOCITY = 0.1              # point-to-point moves between stages
MOVE_ACCEL = 0.1
RASTER_VELOCITY = SCAN_VELOCITY  # the sweep speed IS the technician control
RASTER_ACCEL = 0.05

# The arm does not sweep at the speed it is asked for, and the error is not a
# constant factor -- it is a curve. Measured on this cell IN SIMULATION:
# commanded, observed. Every speed below goes through it (motion_scaling()),
# so on hardware 10 mm/s is only as right as this table: re-measure it there
# (technician-scan-controls skill) and read the observed speed the dry run and
# every scan report.
ROBOT_VELOCITY_CALIBRATION = [
    (0.0050, 0.0079),
    (0.0100, 0.0156),
    (0.0200, 0.0303),
    (0.0350, 0.0472),
    (0.0500, 0.0529),
]

# --- motion safety ------------------------------------------------------------
# Approved cell limits: at most 50 mm/s away from the part and 10 mm/s near it;
# contact only where the probe has been planned onto the measured surface;
# nothing outside the work volume; the warning light on whenever an automated
# stage moves the arm.
NEAR_PART_SPEED = 0.010          # m/s: the sweep, and every move onto or off the part
# m/s, straight-line moves away from the part. NOT 0.050, the limit itself:
# the calibration table was measured on horizontal sweeps, and other
# directions run faster at the same MoveIt scaling -- park_arm()'s vertical
# rise commanded at 50 mm/s peaks at 87.6 mm/s in simulation, and the Azure
# sweep at 50 mm/s peaks at 74 (p95 56). Both behave like ~1.75 m/s full
# scale, so 30 mm/s (scaling ~0.020) peaks near 35.
#
# 0.040 and not more because the simulated curve above is the only peak
# measurement that exists: at ~1.75 m/s full scale a commanded 40 mm/s peaks
# near 47, which is still inside the approved 50 mm/s away limit even on the
# pessimistic reading. It is the one speed here that could be raised without
# new measurement.
#
# EVERYTHING ELSE NEEDS capture_robot_velocity() ON THE ARM FIRST, and the two
# that matter most are JOINT_MOVE_SCALING and EC_AXIS_SPEED below. Do not raise
# those from a guess: the numbers justifying them are simulated peak speeds, and
# simulation says 0.020 joint scaling already peaks at 55 mm/s -- over the limit.
# The cell may well be slower than that and have room, but "may well be" is not
# a measurement, and the thing being bounded is tip speed near a part.
TRANSIT_SPEED = 0.040

# A contact probe reaches and leaves the surface only along its own axis
# (contact_motion() in surface_path_scan.dpi). Between passes that start within
# EC_SHORT_HOP_M of where the probe is, it lifts EC_LIFT_M; otherwise
# EC_APPROACH_M. With EC_SERPENTINE on, consecutive passes meet end to end, so
# every move between passes is the short hop -- one line spacing across, and the
# lift and descent are the shorter of the two.
EC_LIFT_M = 0.010
EC_APPROACH_M = 0.050
EC_SHORT_HOP_M = 0.030

# The lift off the part and the descent onto it, along the tool axis. Below
# NEAR_PART_SPEED on purpose: the calibration was measured on horizontal
# sweeps, and a vertical move at the same scaling peaks 25% faster in
# simulation -- 12.5 mm/s where 10 was commanded. 0.008 still peaked at
# 11.6-11.9 mm/s there (a scaling of 0.0051), so 0.006: at the measured
# ~2.35 m/s-per-unit-scaling peak of an axial move that is ~9 mm/s.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, RE-DERIVED 2026-10-01, BECAUSE THE ANSWER CHANGED UNDER IT.
# 0.006 m/s converts to a MoveIt velocity scaling of 0.0025385, the lowest in a
# session, and there are 106 of these 10 mm moves per scan. Measured in
# results16 (2026-09-30): the lift and the descent of a pass are logged 11.03 to
# 11.6 s apart at that scaling, so a 10 mm move took about 11.2 s -- an average
# of 0.9 mm/s against the 6.0 commanded -- and the 106 of them came to 1151 s,
# 19.2 min of the run, 30-71% of the cycle on a short pass.
#
# THAT 6.7x SHORTFALL WAS NOT THIS CONSTANT. tari_ros commit 1fe21cf ("Only
# time-stretch the passes that actually need it") found that the planner
# stretched EVERY low-scaling trajectory in time, which divides velocities by k
# and accelerations by k SQUARED; at this scaling k is 10.6, so accelerations
# came out 113x too small and a short move never reached cruise at all. Measured
# on the arm at a commanded 10.0 mm/s, peak tool speed fell from 12.0 mm/s to
# 2.40 and the average from 5.3 to 1.0. That stretch now applies only to paths
# that actually fail TOTG.
#
# SO THE COST TO RE-MEASURE IS A FEW MINUTES, NOT NINETEEN. A 10 mm trapezoid at
# 6 mm/s with RASTER_ACCEL 0.05 m/s^2 is 0.12 s and 0.36 mm of ramp at each end
# -- essentially 10/6 s of cruise -- about 1.7 s of motion, which with the
# service round trip the log also shows puts the 106 moves near 4 min rather
# than 19. INFERRED, not measured: nobody has flown a pass since 1fe21cf.
#
# AND THE REMAINDER DOES NOT BUY A SAFETY ARGUMENT. Raising 6 to 10 mm/s would
# take ~1.7 s of motion to ~1.1 s, about 1 min over a whole scan that runs for
# hours at a 0.3 mm line spacing -- against giving up the only margin there is
# on a vertical move toward a part, on a cell where the axial peak is a
# simulated number and the approved limit near the part is 10 mm/s. NOT CHANGED.
# What to do instead: run capture_robot_velocity() on the arm with an axial
# ladder, and if 0.010 really peaks under 10 mm/s there, raise it on that
# measurement.
EC_AXIS_SPEED = 0.006

# MoveIt velocity SCALING for EVERY automated joint move: into a pass or a
# sweep row, back to home, to a named pose. A joint move's tip speed is not a
# Cartesian limit, so this comes from measurement. At MoveIt's default 0.1 the
# probe tip peaks, in simulation, at 132 mm/s going to over_table, 194 in the
# Azure sweep, 245 in the Keyence raster (46 mm above the part) and 75-80
# between eddy-current passes. At 0.02 the peaks are 35 mm/s to over_table and
# 55 in the Keyence stage's row approaches, still over 50, so 0.015. The dry
# run and every sim stage report the peak; see the first-run doc.
JOINT_MOVE_SCALING = 0.015

# UR digital output 0 is this cell's warning light (output 1 is the Keyence
# laser). Every stage that moves the arm holds it on and switches it off
# afterwards, errors included. None = no light.
WARNING_LIGHT_PIN = 0
# Refuse to move when the light cannot be switched on. None means "yes on
# hardware"; simulation has no UR I/O controller to switch it with.
WARNING_LIGHT_REQUIRED = None

# Where the arm goes to look for the part, and where it is left at the end: a
# named state in the cell's SRDF (tari_cell_ex_ur10e_1). None = do not move
# there.
DETECT_POSE = "over_table"
PARK_POSE = "over_table"

# --- toolpath -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Between passes. 0.3 mm is the real-cell eddy-current value (0.1 mm for higher
# resolution); the ultrasound transducer's was 10 mm. At 0.3 mm a whole part is
# hours: preview_ec_scan() says how many, and EC_SCAN_REGION is how to scan an
# area that fits in the time the plan allows.
SCAN_LINE_SPACING = 0.0003
NOETHER_LINE_SPACING = SCAN_LINE_SPACING
NOETHER_MESH = None              # None asks obtain_specimen_mesh()
NOETHER_MESH_OFFSET = (0.0, 0.0, 0.0)
# The tool axis follows the surface normal: on a curved part the probe has to
# stay perpendicular to it. The spring fixture gives some compliance; tilt is a
# known problem, not solved here.
NOETHER_ORIENTATION = "surface"
# Do not ask the planner to snake. It keeps no sign of its own, so the direction
# of every pass has to be decided on this side regardless (EC_TRAVEL_DIRECTION
# and EC_SERPENTINE below), and asking twice only makes it harder to tell what
# ordered the path.
NOETHER_SNAKE = False
# Which way the passes run. With no offset principal_axis_noether's lines follow
# the part's SECOND-longest dimension, and on a curved part that sweeps the
# probe round the curvature inside every pass. pi/2 turns them onto the longest,
# which is x for the specimens in this cell. noether_cut_axes() in
# noether_path.dpi states the rule once, from the planner's own source;
# noether_cut_axis_test.py pins it.
NOETHER_ROTATION_OFFSET = np.pi/2
# And the direction of the FIRST pass along them, -x. The planner keeps no sign
# of its own, so without this it changes from plan to plan.
EC_TRAVEL_DIRECTION = (-1.0, 0.0, 0.0)
# RASTER, NOT COMB. Every pass after the first starts at whichever of its ends is
# nearer to where the previous pass finished, so the move between passes is one
# line spacing sideways instead of the whole length of the part. The probe still
# lifts, transits in the air and descends between passes, but over one line
# spacing that is a short hop (EC_SHORT_HOP_M) rather than a full approach, and
# the transit itself is millimetres. On an 81-pass plan the per-pass overhead was
# 6.8 minutes of a 19.5 minute scan; nearly all of it was the return.
#
# Set this False for a comb -- every pass running along EC_TRAVEL_DIRECTION -- if
# the reading depends on the direction of travel. A probe dragged in contact
# leans against its travel in the spring fixture, so alternating the direction
# alternates the lean, and therefore the lift-off, which stripes the image line
# by line. A comb pays for the return in order to measure every line the same
# way.
EC_SERPENTINE = True
# The probe's rotation about its own axis is LOCKED for the scan: every pose is
# one reference orientation tilted onto the surface normal, never twisted.
# "fixed" twists with diagonal tilt and
# flips 180 degrees near the +x end of a cylinder; see regularize_tool_roll().
NOETHER_TOOL_ROLL = "locked"
# That reference's tool x axis, in the world, for a probe pointing straight
# down. +x is close to how this arm holds its tooling over the bench. Set it
# from the cable-envelope test: the direction that leaves wrist_3 mid-range
# with the Azure and Keyence cables slack.
NOETHER_ROLL_REFERENCE = (1.0, 0.0, 0.0)
# HOW FAR THE PROBE IS TURNED ABOUT ITS OWN AXIS, in degrees, held constant for
# the whole scan.
#
# WHY IT IS A SETTING. The probe is a split-D differential coil, and a split-D is
# DIRECTIONAL: it responds to a crack perpendicular to its split and is nearly
# blind to one parallel to it. So the rotation is a measurement parameter the
# technician chooses for the flaw being looked for, not something for the planner
# to inherit from whichever way the raster happens to run.
#
# WHAT ZERO MEANS. At 0 the probe's SPLIT lies along NOETHER_ROLL_REFERENCE in
# the PART's frame -- world +x in this cell -- with the probe axis vertical.
# POSITIVE turns the split anticlockwise about the probe's own axis seen from
# outside the part (right-hand rule about the outward surface normal). The same
# number holds at every waypoint of every pass, in both serpentine directions:
# the "locked" roll builds every pose by swinging ONE reference orientation onto
# the surface normal, and a swing adds no rotation about the tool's own axis, so
# the rotation is in the construction rather than applied to it.
#
# set_probe_rotation(degrees) changes it from the prompt, and refuses one the
# cables cannot hold before anything moves.
EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG = 0.0
# A FALLBACK, and only that: the wrist_3 angle the arm holds at the zero datum
# with the probe vertical, in degrees.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT IS PREFERRED AND IS USED WHEN IT EXISTS.
# capture_cable_envelope() records this angle as `wrist3_start_deg` on every row
# of the cable-envelope file, in the same joint state it measures that row's band
# from -- so the band and the datum are one self-consistent pair, and
# check_probe_rotation() takes both from the envelope. It also takes the datum AT
# EACH POSE'S TILT, which this constant cannot express: reaching a tilt turns the
# wrist on the way, and across this cell's eight samples the datum runs 147 to 179
# degrees.
#
# This value is for a cell whose envelope predates that field, or one measured
# without it. It is the untilted number only, and being a transcription it goes
# stale silently the moment the tooling is unbolted -- which is exactly why the
# measurement wins. This cell's reads 166.09 deg against an untilted band of
# +16.09..+301.34, so it holds -150..+135 deg of rotation with the probe vertical.
#
# The rotation lands on this joint one degree for one degree, which is what lets
# check_probe_rotation() refuse a rotation without solving any IK. None, with no
# measurement either, makes the check say UNCHECKED rather than pass silently.
EC_PROBE_ROTATION_WRIST3_DEG = 166.09
NOETHER_UV_METHOD = "planar"
# THE PROBE TIP ON THE MEASURED SURFACE. probe_end on the ec tooling is the
# tip: ec_probe -> probe_end is 68.07 mm along the probe axis and the probe mesh
# ends at 68.32 mm (tari_ur_description urdf/ec/tooling_macro.xacro and
# meshes/ec_probe.stl). A nonzero offset here is a standoff, which an
# eddy-current scan does not want.
NOETHER_Z_OFFSET = 0.0

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTACT PRELOAD -- how hard the probe is pressed into the part
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How far PAST the measured surface the tip is commanded, along its own axis, in
# METRES. The spring fixture takes up the difference, so this is the number that
# loads the spring and keeps the probe face on the part over the millimetre-scale
# error in the measured mesh.
#
# CHANGE IT FROM THE PROMPT, NOT HERE:
#
#     set_ec_preload(0.0003)     # 0.30 mm into the part
#     ec_preload()               # what is in force now
#
# A bare `EC_PRELOAD_M = 0.0003` typed at the prompt does NOT reach the shared
# globals: it reads back unchanged and the next plan uses the old value.
# set_ec_preload() is the only way in, it validates, and it prints what it set.
#
# METRES, ALWAYS. There is no millimetre setter, because one function that took
# either unit could be handed 0.3 for "0.3 mm" and command 300. It prints the
# millimetre equivalent instead, so the number can still be read back the way
# the fixture is measured.
#
# 0 at startup: the right value is a property of this fixture and this part, and
# nothing here has measured it. At 0 the tip is commanded exactly onto the
# measured surface and every error in the mesh becomes lift-off (mesh high) or
# compression (mesh low), one for one.
EC_PRELOAD_M = 0.0

# The hard ceiling on the preload, in metres. Refused above this, by
# set_ec_preload() and by plan_ec_path(preload=) alike.
#
# TWO NUMBERS OUT OF THE TOOLING DESCRIPTION SET IT (tari_ur_description
# urdf/ec/tooling_macro.xacro and the meshes it names):
#
#   * The compliance is probe_holder, a 12.5 mm radius plunger, sliding in
#     bearing_tube, a 16.0 mm bore. Both joints are FIXED in the URDF, so the
#     spring travel is not modelled at all. The one length in the description
#     that bears on the slide is the overlap between the two at rest:
#     probe_holder is mounted 20.0 mm up a 24.5 mm bearing tube, so 4.5 mm of
#     plunger is inside it. A preload of the same order as that is one that
#     changes the fixture's geometry rather than just loading its spring; 2 mm
#     is under half of it.
#   * probe_end, the commanded tip, is 68.07 mm along the probe and the probe
#     mesh ends at 68.32 mm. Past 0.25 mm of preload the modelled probe body is
#     already inside the part, so every clearance number the planner reports is
#     optimistic by the preload. 2 mm is as far past that as is worth allowing.
#
# Beyond a couple of millimetres the arm stops pressing a spring and starts
# pushing the part: the fixture bottoms out, the reading saturates, and the
# probe or the specimen is what gives. To gain margin, shrink the scan area --
# never press harder.
EC_PRELOAD_CEILING_M = 0.002
# Scan only this part of the toolpath: (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax) in world
# metres, or None for everything on the part. Waypoints outside are dropped and
# passes split where they leave it: the scan area shrinks, the motion never
# grows beyond the planned surface.
EC_SCAN_REGION = None
# LET THE OPERATOR DRAW THE REGION, after the Azure rough mesh and before the
# Keyence raster pays for it. A 3D window opens on the rough surface with the
# detector's region already selected, so the job is to CORRECT a box rather
# than draw one; Accept hands its bounding region to stages 3 and 5.
#
# On by default because the detector is not good enough to be trusted alone: it
# has returned 135 x 287 mm for an 83 x 165 mm plate. Everything else inside
# such a box -- breadboard, clamps, pins, a stepped mount block -- is then
# measured, meshed and rastered, for a toolpath an order of magnitude larger
# than the part needs and tool poses near 90 degrees off vertical where Noether
# goes over a rim.
#
# False for an unattended run. So is run_automated_scan(roi_select=False), and
# a session with no usable display skips it by itself with a printed reason
# rather than stopping -- an automated scan must still run with nobody there.
ROI_SELECT = True
# The steepest a pose may lean from vertical, in degrees. Beyond it a waypoint
# is dropped and its pass split, like a region crop -- a contact probe is not
# planned down a step wall or round a flank it cannot hold. NOT MEASURED: the
# spring fixture's workable tilt is unknown and 45 is a placeholder. Planned
# tilt reaches 90 degrees at a step in the reconstructed surface -- 9.6 mm is
# enough. None = no limit.
EC_MAX_TILT_DEG = 45.0
# The tool axis is averaged along each pass over this much travel before the
# roll is locked; positions are not touched, so the tip still goes where the
# mesh says. The probe's face is 5.8 mm across (tari_ur_description
# meshes/ec_probe.stl) and cannot follow the surface's orientation on a finer
# scale, while a reconstructed mesh's normals change on EVERY waypoint -- and
# the arm follows them: in simulation a 9 mm pass over a reconstructed surface
# crawled at 0.3 mm/s against 10 commanded, because at the sweep's MoveIt
# scaling each orientation change is slow. None or 0 = off.
EC_AXIS_SMOOTHING_M = 0.006
# Every pass is resampled to this spacing before it is flown (None = as Noether
# gave it). Noether's waypoints are wherever a slicing plane crossed a mesh
# edge -- anywhere from 0.7 micrometres to 0.5 mm apart, hundreds of thousands
# of them on a reconstruction -- and a uniform 1 mm keeps every sweep request
# even and a quarter to half the size. The sample pitch is the position
# trigger's and does not depend on this.
EC_WAYPOINT_SPACING_M = SCAN_STEP_SIZE
# Clean the mesh before Noether plans over it: drop faces on non-manifold
# edges and components under 1% of the faces (clean_mesh_for_planning() in
# noether_path.dpi). A Poisson-reconstructed mesh routinely has both, and
# Noether refuses such a mesh outright ("Connecting vertex does not exist ...").
# False = plan over the mesh as given.
EC_CLEAN_PLANNING_MESH = True

# What ONE Noether waypoint costs, so a plan can be priced before it is made.
# All three measured on this stack, not assumed:
#   * spacing: 911 511 poses over 383.648 m of raw toolpath -> 0.42 mm between
#     waypoints. Noether sets it from the mesh, so it is a property of the
#     reconstruction's resolution, not a setting here; re-measure it if the
#     planner or the mesh pipeline changes.
#   * bytes: a geometry_msgs/Pose costs 316 bytes of Python objects on this Iron
#     install (measured over 200 000 of them), and the planner replies with one
#     per waypoint. That reply is the single largest allocation in the planning
#     stage.
#   * gate: 1.7 ms per waypoint of the finished toolpath -- 116 851 waypoints of
#     a contact toolpath over a 198 422-triangle specimen reconstruction in
#     194 s on an 8-core dev host, 16.2% of the poses measured and the rest
#     certified, 366 MB peak. Two things it depends on: cores (the distance
#     kernel is threaded -- see SURFACE_QUERY_WORKERS) and how much of the path
#     runs close to the limit, since that is what the sampler refines into. The
#     cell PC is slower than the dev host, so leave headroom. Re-measure it if
#     the tooling changes; it is a rate, not a law.
NOETHER_POSE_SPACING_M = 0.00042
NOETHER_POSE_REPLY_BYTES = 316
EC_GATE_SECONDS_PER_POSE = 0.0017

# What a plan has to fit in before plan_ec_path() will build it. These bound the
# PLANNING stage; SCAN_LIMITS bounds the scan that follows. Separate because
# they fail differently and at different times: a scan that is too long wastes
# hours, a plan that is too large takes the dgpy process out -- 8.4 GB and an
# OOM kill -- and with it every stage already paid for.
EC_PLAN_LIMITS = {
    "max_plan_poses": 1500000,
    "max_plan_memory_bytes": 2*(1 << 30),
    "max_plan_seconds": 1800.0,
}

# Run the tooling-clearance gate over the eddy-current toolpath. It is the only
# thing standing between a toolpath that places the PROBE correctly and a
# Keyence head or an Azure bracket pressed into the part -- the Keyence stage
# has done exactly that. Off is a deliberate decision for a bench test
# with no specimen, never a way to make planning finish sooner: the gate samples
# (TOOLING_GATE_SAMPLE_ABOVE) rather than measuring 180 000 poses one at a time.
EC_CHECK_CLEARANCE = True

# PLAN THE KEYENCE RASTER OVER THE AZURE ROUGH MESH EVEN WHEN THE TWO DISAGREE.
# Stage 2a of run_automated_scan() gauges the rough mesh against the Keyence and
# refuses stage 3 when they are more than AZURE_GAUGE_MAX_MM apart, because that
# disagreement is subtracted straight off the profiler's standoff: a 13.6 mm
# disagreement has cost 26-39% of a 10 inch plate. This is the deliberate "I
# know, fly it anyway" for an unattended run -- run_automated_scan(allow_azure_bias=True)
# is the same override for one call. Same shape and same meaning as
# SPECIMEN_ALLOW_DEAD_ZONE below.
EC_ALLOW_AZURE_BIAS = False

# --- projection ---------------------------------------------------------------
# "live" projects each reading as it is taken, at the cost of a second
# serialised globalrev wait per reading. "deferred" records everything and
# replays the trace list afterwards for an identical image, much faster.
PROJECTION_MODE = "live"
PROJECTION_PIXELS = None         # None derives it from the step size
PROJECTION_SPOT_RADIUS = None
PROJECTION_TEXTURED_PART = True

# --- reconstruction -----------------------------------------------------------
SPECIMEN_SOURCE = "auto"
SPECIMEN_REGION = None           # None uses the detector, or the work volume
SPECIMEN_CLEAN_MESH = False
# THE STANDOFF DECISION. The Azure's depth mode has a minimum RANGE, a camera
# flown inside it degrades rather than fails, and the sweep therefore has to
# hold that range. It does NOT have to hold it as height: standing off to the
# side and aiming back at the part buys the same distance at a height the arm
# can reach. Which is the point, because the height a straight-down sweep needs
# is not reachable here -- camera 1.455, probe_end 1.380, floor + 455 mm, and
# solved against this cell's own kinematics that has NO IK solution at all over
# the far x column (x >= 1.145), and only 84.6% of the work volume anywhere.
#
# So the mode below still decides the range, and SPECIMEN_SWEEP_STYLE decides
# how the range is bought:
#
#   "pan"   (default) hold the range, aim the camera, cover the region from
#           several directions. The camera height is chosen off a ladder whose
#           TOP RUNG IS VERTICAL -- height equal to the slant range, so the
#           lateral stand-off is zero and the tool hangs straight down. That
#           rung is tried first and, at ex-ur10e-1 with a 198 x 143 mm part,
#           it is the one that flies: 8 of 8 directions admit a roll, the tilt
#           is 8-13 degrees (against 22-29 at the rung below, outside every tilt
#           the cable envelope has been measured at), and probe_end lands at
#           1.421-1.429 -- 65 mm under the MOTION envelope. The
#           lateral rungs below it are still there for a sweep that needs real
#           parallax; see AZURE_PAN_VERTICAL in mesh_reconstruction.dpi.
#   "dome"  a straight-down raster, height derived from the mode:
#             NFOV_UNBINNED   0.50 m minimum -> 1.486 over a 31 mm part
#             WFOV_2X2BINNED  0.25 m minimum -> 1.236
#           Same geometry as the vertical pan rung, and with rot=False it holds
#           ONE orientation -- the one the arm is already standing in, so in
#           the cable band by construction -- and asks for NO wrist rotation at
#           all. Reachability and the cable band are both checked at plan time,
#           and a grid whose corners do not reach is pulled in toward its
#           centre rather than refused, because at this standoff every stop
#           sees the whole part and the raster is parallax rather than
#           coverage. THIS IS WHAT STAGE 2 FLIES (see reconstruct_rough).
#           The pan sweep is for a part whose SIDES matter and can be reached.
#
# DO NOT reach for WFOV to fly lower. The eddy-current probe and its holder sit
# 65-110 mm from the camera, 45.5 degrees off the optical axis. In NFOV's
# 75 x 65 degrees they clear the bottom of the frame by 11.8 degrees; in WFOV's
# 120 x 120 they are 15.7 degrees INSIDE it and occupy 2.9% of every frame, at a
# distance no depth mode can measure. The tooling is rigid to the camera, so no
# sweep can be planned around it. Computed from the rendered URDF.
#
# Changing the mode here without changing the driver's (ur_moveit.launch.py,
# depth_mode:) is the one way left to get this wrong, so the bring-up's sensor
# report prints both, and the depth image's SIZE is checked against it.
SPECIMEN_DEPTH_MODE = "NFOV_UNBINNED"
# "dome", AND IT IS DECLARED HERE RATHER THAN FORCED INSIDE reconstruct_rough().
# check_sweep_plan() resolves the style from THIS global, so a stage that
# overrides it with a kwarg default makes the pre-flight grade a sweep that is
# not the one the arm goes on to fly.
#
# See reconstruct_rough() for why the dome is right for this stage: the pan
# sweep orbits the part to see its SIDES, and the sides of a thin flat plate
# cannot be reached from this cell at all.
SPECIMEN_SWEEP_STYLE = "dome"    # "pan" or "dome"
SPECIMEN_ALLOW_DEAD_ZONE = False # True flies inside the minimum anyway

# WHAT THE SWEEP IS FOR, which is what decides how few viewing directions it may
# be flown from before it is REFUSED (AZURE_SWEEP_PURPOSES):
#
#   "planning"  1 direction. A height field, which is exactly what Noether needs
#               to raster the top of a part and what the Keyence pass refines.
#   "closed"    AZURE_PAN_MIN_DIRECTIONS (2). A surface meant to close over the
#               part -- metrology, real relief, anything measured rather than
#               planned over.
#
# None leaves it to each caller. run_automated_scan()'s stage 2 goes through
# reconstruct_rough(), which passes "planning" itself, so this only has to be
# set to hold that stage to the STRICTER rule. Setting it to "planning" here
# extends the looser rule to every Azure sweep in the session, capture scenarios
# included, which is not what you want on a measurement take.
SPECIMEN_SWEEP_PURPOSE = None

# The top of the part, world z. None takes the detector's measured surface_z for
# the region it detected, and falls back to the work volume's floor when nothing
# measured it. THE SWEEP HEIGHT AND THE RANGE CHECK BOTH COME OFF THIS PLANE, so
# a part whose top the detector cannot see -- a dark or shiny one -- is worth
# setting by hand: at the floor, a 31 mm part is flown 31 mm inside the depth
# mode's minimum and the check that exists to catch that measures from the floor
# too and passes it.
SPECIMEN_SURFACE_Z = None

# -- pan sweep. None everywhere means "derive it", which is the intent.
SPECIMEN_SWEEP_SLANT = None      # None = the depth mode's minimum + 30 mm
SPECIMEN_SWEEP_CAMERA_HEIGHT = None  # None walks the ladder, vertical rung first
SPECIMEN_SWEEP_DIRECTIONS = 4    # of the 8 candidates, the reachable ones
SPECIMEN_PAN_STOPS = (4, 2)      # aim points per direction; sets the frame count

# -- dome sweep, used when SPECIMEN_SWEEP_STYLE = "dome".
SPECIMEN_SWEEP_HEIGHT = None     # None derives it from SPECIMEN_DEPTH_MODE
SPECIMEN_SWEEP_STOPS = (5, 5)
KEYENCE_BACKEND = "pc"           # tari_pc_reconstruction; see the Phase B map
# ==============================================================================
# HOW THE SENSORS ARE POINTED -- ONE VOCABULARY, TWO SENSORS, LIVE
# ==============================================================================
# Both of these are set at the prompt with set_sensor_orientation() and both are
# printed in the startup banner. The vocabulary is shared so that "normal" means
# the same thing to either sensor:
#
#   "z"       LOCKED TO WORLD Z. The tool axis is vertical for every waypoint,
#             whatever the surface does. For the Keyence that also puts the laser
#             line across the direction of travel, which is the only roll that
#             sweeps area. This is what has always been flown here.
#   "normal"  FOLLOW THE SPECIMEN'S SURFACE. The tool axis is the surface normal,
#             capped, and -- for the Keyence -- with a per-waypoint fallback to
#             the "z" pose wherever the normal pose is refused, so the stage
#             degrades instead of failing. See KEYENCE_NORMAL_* below.
#   "orbit"   AZURE ONLY. Fly the part from several azimuths (the pan sweep), so
#             the TSDF closes over the sides instead of producing a height field.
#             Pre-checked before anything moves; see reconstruct_rough().
#
# The older spellings still work and mean what they always did: "fixed" is "z",
# and "surface"/"capped"/"auto" are passed through to
# _KeyenceReconstruction.ORIENT untouched.

#: How the Azure rough sweep is pointed. "z" is the straight-down dome at one
#: fixed orientation -- what this cell has flown since the pan sweep was found
#: to orbit a plate whose sides cannot be reached. See reconstruct_rough() for
#: the full history and for what "orbit" costs.
AZURE_ORIENTATION = "z"

#: What happens when "orbit" is asked for and the part cannot be orbited.
#: False REPORTS IT AND FLIES THE LOCKED-Z DOME, which is the whole reason the
#: orbit was taken out in the first place: a pan sweep refused at all 8 azimuths
#: and all 6 heights takes out every stage downstream, and the rough mesh that
#: the Keyence raster and the eddy-current toolpath both need is never made.
#: True refuses the stage instead, for a part whose sides are the measurement.
AZURE_ORBIT_REQUIRED = False

# How the Keyence is pointed over its raster. "z" (tool z vertical, the laser
# line across the direction of travel) is the default and is what has been flown.
# "normal" follows the rough mesh's normals with a per-waypoint fallback.
# "surface" is the raw normal-following mode with no fallback, and on a strongly
# curved part it can fail to reach even the first pose. See
# _KeyenceReconstruction.ORIENT in mesh_reconstruction.dpi.
KEYENCE_ORIENTATION = "z"

# --- the Keyence "normal" mode's guards --------------------------------------
# WHY THERE ARE GUARDS AT ALL. Tilting the profiler is the right thing for the
# measurement -- on results16's row 2 the laser line spanned 21.5 mm of height
# across a CURVED surface and 20.4% of the returns were within 5 mm of the
# sensor's far limit -- and it is also the one change in this pipeline that can
# put the EDDY-CURRENT PROBE into the part. probe_end sits ~73 mm to one side of
# the optical frame on the same mounting plate and the barrel is 7.1 mm in
# radius, so a tilt that stands the sensor off correctly can swing the probe
# down into a curved flank. motion.py and tari_cpp_mtc both carry that warning.
#
# So "normal" is a per-waypoint choice between the normal pose and the "z" pose,
# and the "z" pose is the fallback rather than a failure. Four things can send a
# waypoint back to vertical, and all four are cheap enough to ask about every
# waypoint of an 8500-pose raster:
#
#   tilt       past KEYENCE_NORMAL_MAX_TILT_DEG. More tilt is more IK failures
#              and more refused passes, and the cable band narrows sharply with
#              it -- measured 285 deg of wrist-3 travel at no tilt, 90 deg at
#              -40 deg of tilt.
#   cable      the measured wrist-3 band AT THAT TILT does not admit the roll
#              this path holds. Absolute wrist-3 angle, never resolved by +/-360:
#              two angles a turn apart are one tool orientation and two very
#              different cable states.
#   clearance  the tooling envelope comes within KEYENCE_NORMAL_CLEARANCE_M of
#              the mesh. This is the probe-into-the-part case.
#   standoff   the commanded distance leaves the guarded measuring window.
#
# A waypoint whose VERTICAL pose also fails is dropped by the clearance gate
# exactly as it is today; nothing here loosens that.
KEYENCE_ORIENT_FALLBACK = True
#: Degrees from vertical. The tilt ladder's own rungs are (0, 15, 30, 45) and
#: this is the ceiling the per-waypoint choice applies on top of whichever rung
#: was selected. 30 rather than 45: at -40 deg of tilt the measured wrist-3 band
#: is 90 deg wide, which is not enough room to fly a raster in.
KEYENCE_NORMAL_MAX_TILT_DEG = 30.0
#: Metres of tooling clearance a tilted waypoint must leave against the planning
#: mesh. None means TOOLING_CLEARANCE_M, the cell-wide default the untilted
#: raster is already held to.
KEYENCE_NORMAL_CLEARANCE_M = None
#: Degrees of wrist-3 travel the measured cable band must STILL leave at a
#: waypoint's tilt, or that waypoint goes back to vertical. The IK branch for a
#: pass is chosen once for the whole pass, so a narrower band is no room to
#: choose in. Against this cell's measured envelope -- 285 deg untilted, 240 at
#: +40 deg about x, 150 at -20, 90 at -40 -- 120 admits tilts out to about
#: -25 deg about x and the whole of the +x and y ranges.
KEYENCE_NORMAL_MIN_BAND_DEG = 120.0
#: Waypoints a run of one orientation choice must be at least, or it is absorbed
#: into its neighbours. Without it a mask that crosses a gate's limit back and
#: forth tilts the tool on and off along a pass, which is a full orientation step
#: between waypoints 1 mm apart -- not a collision and not a cable fault, but a
#: Cartesian path whose IK branch can change mid-pass, which costs the row at
#: min_fraction and reads as a reach problem. 8 at a 1 mm step is 8 mm of pass.
KEYENCE_NORMAL_MIN_RUN = 8

# --- the synthesised test plate, for working without a real specimen ----------
TEST_PLATE_SIZE = 0.30
TEST_PLATE_HEIGHT = 1.00
TEST_PLATE_BOW = 0.03
TEST_PLATE_DIVISIONS = 61

# --- plane-scan settings, unused here ----------------------------------------
# An eddy-current scan follows a surface; there is no rectangular grid. These
# exist because the shared modules read them.
SCAN_M = None
SCAN_N = None
SCAN_ORIGIN = (0.75, -0.35)

# --- recording ----------------------------------------------------------------
RECORD_TRACE_LIST = True
# Where scans are written. NOT a hardcoded home directory: the cell PC runs
# under a different account and writes to /data/scandata, so a path like
# "/home/user/scandata" is unwritable there and a session dies with
# PermissionError before anything can be recorded. Same resolution order as
# the capture tool's CAPTURE_ROOT (cell_capture_node.dpi): what the site says,
# then the mount the cell actually has, then this account's own home.
SCAN_STORAGE_DIR = (os.environ.get("TARI_SCANDATA")
                    or os.environ.get("SCANDATA_ROOT")
                    or ("/data/scandata" if os.path.isdir("/data/scandata")
                        else os.path.expanduser("~/scandata")))

# NOT USED BY EDDY CURRENT. A gate selects a time window of a waveform, and an
# impedance reading has no time axis -- the equivalent operation is the
# null/rotation/gain conditioning, which is a compiled math function. Empty
# rather than absent because the shared modules read the name.
CSCAN_GATES = []

# Where a waveform digitizer would publish. There is no digitizer here; this
# names the eddy-current instrument's real channel so anything that reports it
# names something that exists.
ASCAN_CHANNEL_PATH = EC_REAL_PATH

# Trace lists are large and the recording database keeps them in memory unless
# told otherwise. A file-backed storage manager puts them on disk, which is what
# makes a long scan survivable; the plan's memory check asks whether this exists.
if SCAN_STORAGE_DIR is not None:
    os.makedirs(SCAN_STORAGE_DIR, exist_ok=True)
    scan_storage_manager = snde.setup_file_storage_manager(recdb, SCAN_STORAGE_DIR)
else:
    scan_storage_manager = None

# Read by the shared plane-scan code, which an eddy-current scan does not use.
RASTER_MIN_FRACTION = 1.0

# The ultrasound modules' idea of "the instrument". Left None: this config's
# instrument is `ec_source`, and the eddy-current scan passes it explicitly
# rather than through this global. Defined so that anything reporting on it
# finds a name rather than raising.
global ascan_source
ascan_source = None

global SCAN_MODULE_DIR
SCAN_MODULE_DIR = _scan_module_dir()

# FIRST, because everything else reports through it. progress.dpi is the shared
# "say what is happening during the slow parts" helper: rate-limited plain
# lines with elapsed time, a fraction, an honest ETA and a closing line that
# prints even when the stage is killed. It depends on nothing, so it goes at
# the top of the list; the mesh reconstruction, the Noether planning and the
# specimen stages all call into it.
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "progress.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "work_cell.dpi"))
# The cable envelope: how far wrist 3 may turn before the end-of-arm loom binds,
# PER TILT, as capture_cable_envelope() measured it. Loaded next to work_cell.dpi
# because it is the same kind of thing -- a measured property of this cell, read
# from the manifest, that everything downstream has to respect -- and loaded
# EARLY because the guard built on it (install_cable_guard(), at the bottom of
# this file) is on for the whole session rather than only inside a capture
# scenario.
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "cable_envelope.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "toolpath_segments.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "trace_acquisition.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "surface_projection.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "triggered_acquisition.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "noether_path.dpi"))
# The independent tooling-clearance check. Loaded next to noether_path.dpi
# because it is the same kind of thing -- geometry over a planned path, no ROS
# and no robot -- and because mesh_scan.dpi and mesh_reconstruction.dpi both
# refuse to fly a sweep without it (they warn and continue if it is absent).
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "tooling_clearance.dpi"))
# THE C++ BVH BACKEND for SurfaceDistance.signed_distance(), swapped in over
# the numpy one. Must come AFTER tooling_clearance.dpi (it replaces a method on
# the class that file defines) and BEFORE anything builds a SurfaceDistance.
#
# It is faster and it is MORE correct, both measured rather than claimed: the
# numpy path stops widening its candidate set at max_k and can return a
# distance up to 0.19 mm LARGER than the truth -- the optimistic direction --
# on about one point in ten thousand, while the BVH matches brute force over
# every triangle exactly. It also declines to prune on exact_within, because an
# unsigned cap can report a POSITIVE number for a point that is inside the
# surface; that costs it roughly 4x and can only make the gate more
# conservative.
#
# If the kernel cannot be built or loaded -- no compiler, a read-only cache --
# this prints one line and leaves the numpy implementation exactly as it was.
# Set SURFACE_DISTANCE_FAST_REQUIRED = True to make that an error instead,
# which is what an acceptance run should do: "silently fell back to the slow
# path" is otherwise indistinguishable from "was fast".
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "surface_distance_fast.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "pitch_crossings.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "timestamped_trigger.dpi"))
# WHAT MAKES AN EDDY-CURRENT SCAN SURVIVE THIS PROCESS. The recording database
# is in memory: before this file existed an EC scan wrote nothing to disk at any
# point, and on 2026-10-02 a run of 59 completed passes was lost in full when
# the process was killed at pass 60 of 64. One npz per completed pass now goes
# beside the meshes, and replay_ec_scan() rebuilds the projected image from
# those files in any later session -- with the conditioning rotation overridable,
# which is the half that cannot be done from a conditioned scalar.
#
# BEFORE ec_acquisition.dpi, which is what drives it.
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "ec_trace_store.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "ec_acquisition.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "scan_parameters.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "scan_plan.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "scan_accumulation.dpi"))

# THE CELL'S MEASURED SPEED CURVE REPLACES THE ONE ABOVE, when the manifest has
# one. Here, after scan_parameters.dpi and scan_plan.dpi, because those are what
# consume it -- and before anything plans, so nothing converts a speed through the
# transcribed table first. The config's own value stays as
# ROBOT_VELOCITY_CALIBRATION_CONFIG.
adopt_measured_velocity_curve()
# The Keyence's measuring window, and the three checks that keep the sensor
# inside it. Same shape as tooling_clearance.dpi above -- geometry over a
# planned path plus a cheap watch on the sensor's own returns -- and
# mesh_reconstruction.dpi warns and continues if it is absent. See its header
# for the kind of raster it exists to refuse.
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "keyence_standoff.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "mesh_reconstruction.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "part_detection.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "specimen.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "mesh_scan.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "surface_path_scan.dpi"))
# Getting out of a state a previous run left the arm in. AFTER
# surface_path_scan.dpi, because the retreat it flies is that file's
# contact_lift()/contact_retract() -- the same tool-axis move a pass entry uses.
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "start_state_recovery.dpi"))
# Reads ROI_SELECT, set above, for its default.
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "roi_select.dpi"))

# The limits a scan plan is checked against. DEFAULT_LIMITS is defined by
# scan_parameters.dpi and carries the measured numbers with the comments saying
# where each came from, so this has to come after the includes.
#
# NO OVERRIDES. Every one of those numbers was measured on the ultrasound
# pipeline, and the eddy-current path has not been characterised: its readings
# are 16 bytes rather than tens of kilobytes, so the memory ceiling is far from
# binding, but the trace-RATE ceiling is a property of the staging pipeline and
# is the one that will actually bite. Re-measure before relaxing it.
global SCAN_LIMITS
SCAN_LIMITS = dict(DEFAULT_LIMITS)

# The Azure dome sweep's ceiling is the transit speed, not the module's 50 mm/s
# (see TRANSIT_SPEED: a sweep commanded at 50 peaked at 74). Coverage does not
# suffer -- it is set by the motion gate over path length, not by speed.
ReconstructionSource.MAX_SWEEP_VELOCITY = min(ReconstructionSource.MAX_SWEEP_VELOCITY,
                                              TRANSIT_SPEED)

# ==============================================================================
# INSTRUMENT SETUP
# ==============================================================================

global daq
global ec_source
global ec_cond
global ec_model
daq = None
ec_source = None
ec_cond = None
# The response-table model, when EC_SOURCE == "sim" and a table was named.
# None in every other case, including "sim" with no table -- so "is there a
# model?" is one test everywhere and not a question about EC_SOURCE.
ec_model = None

if EC_SOURCE == "hardware":
    try:
        import dgpython_nidaqmx as daqmx
        daq = daqmx.ai_voltage('daq', recdb,
                               ["%s/%s" % (EC_DAQ_DEVICE, c) for c in EC_CHANNELS])
        print("  eddy-current DAQ on %s and %s" % (EC_REAL_PATH, EC_IMAG_PATH))
    except Exception as _e:
        # Not fatal. Everything except run_ec_scan() works without the card, and
        # a machine with no NI-DAQ is the normal case for working on the
        # reconstruction and planning stages.
        daq = None
        print("  NO eddy-current DAQ (%s: %s)" % (type(_e).__name__, _e))
        print("  reconstruction and planning still work; run_ec_scan() will refuse.")
elif EC_SOURCE == "sim":
    include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "sim_ec_source.dpi"))

    # The response-table model, if a table and a plate were named. Built BEFORE
    # the source, because the source needs it; and failing to build it is not
    # fatal -- the synthetic signal still runs, and a scan that silently used
    # synthetic numbers while the operator believed it was using the table
    # would be far worse than one that says so here.
    ec_model = None
    if EC_TABLE and EC_PLATE:
        try:
            import ec_response_table as _ec_table_mod
            import ec_plate_model as _ec_plate_mod
        except ImportError:
            # include() puts the .dpi's own path on sys.path, not the module
            # directory; the config knows where that is.
            if SCAN_MODULE_DIR and SCAN_MODULE_DIR not in sys.path:
                sys.path.insert(0, SCAN_MODULE_DIR)
            import ec_response_table as _ec_table_mod
            import ec_plate_model as _ec_plate_mod
        try:
            _ec_tbl = _ec_table_mod.ECResponseTable(EC_TABLE)
            ec_model = _ec_plate_mod.ECPlateModel(
                _ec_tbl, _ec_plate_mod.load_plate(EC_PLATE),
                coil_axis_deg=EC_COIL_AXIS_DEG,
                v_per_ohm=EC_SIM_V_PER_OHM,
                pose_fn=lambda: live_pose.snapshot())
        except Exception as _e:
            ec_model = None
            print("  ** NO response-table model (%s: %s)"
                  % (type(_e).__name__, _e))
            print("     Falling back to the SYNTHETIC signal. Nothing this "
                  "scan produces comes from the table.")
    elif EC_TABLE or EC_PLATE:
        # One without the other is always a mistake, and a silent fallback here
        # is the kind that is noticed a week later.
        print("  ** EC_TABLE and EC_PLATE must BOTH be set to use the response "
              "table (table=%r plate=%r). Using the SYNTHETIC signal."
              % (EC_TABLE, EC_PLATE))

    daq = SimECSource(recdb, transaction_clock, EC_REAL_PATH, EC_IMAG_PATH,
                      position_fn=lambda: probe_position(),
                      pose_fn=(None if ec_model is None
                               else (lambda: live_pose.snapshot())),
                      model=ec_model,
                      sample_rate=EC_SIM_SAMPLE_RATE,
                      block_hz=EC_SIM_BLOCK_HZ)
    print("  ** SIMULATED eddy-current instrument on %s and %s "
          "(%.0f Hz in blocks of %d)"
          % (EC_REAL_PATH, EC_IMAG_PATH, EC_SIM_SAMPLE_RATE, daq.block_samples))
    if ec_model is None:
        print("     THE NUMBERS ARE SYNTHETIC. This is an architectural "
              "stand-in for the DAQ, not a model of an eddy-current probe -- "
              "nothing it produces is a measurement of anything. Set EC_TABLE "
              "and EC_PLATE to drive it from a generated response table.")
    else:
        for _line in ec_model.describe().splitlines():
            print("     %s" % (_line,))
        if ec_model.table.is_stub:
            print("     ** THE TABLE IS A STUB: its physics is invented. "
                  "Shapes are exercised, numbers mean nothing.")
        else:
            print("     Simulated from the response table. The coil geometry "
                  "behind it is a documented placeholder rather than a fit, so "
                  "SHAPES are meaningful and absolute amplitudes are not.")
elif EC_SOURCE != "none":
    raise ValueError("EC_SOURCE must be 'hardware', 'sim' or 'none', not %r"
                     % (EC_SOURCE,))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE INSTRUMENT ITSELF -- for the null, and only for the null
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The readings come from the DAQ above, not from here. This handle exists so the
# scan can balance the instrument's bridge with the probe on the part; the
# instrument's other settings (frequency, gain, probe drive) are set on its own
# front panel.
#
# OPENING THE DRIVER CHANGES NOTHING ON THE INSTRUMENT. It reads the identity and
# the current settings and stops there, so the instrument can be set up on its
# front panel before or after this config starts, in either order. Nothing here
# ever writes a setting -- the null is the only command this config sends.
#
# THAT DEPENDS ON THE INSTALLED DRIVER, NOT ON THIS FILE. An older
# dgpython-uniwest switches all five instrument channels off as it opens, and the
# instrument then looks correctly configured on its front panel while the DAQ
# reads nothing. A flat scan with a healthy-looking instrument is that: check the
# channel is on, and check the installed driver against the source (the startup
# banner prints the version and the path of everything loaded).

global ec_instrument
ec_instrument = None


global connect_ec_instrument
def connect_ec_instrument(port=None, baud=None, name="uniwest"):
    """Open the eddy-current instrument so its null can be issued. Not fatal.

    Leaves the handle in `ec_instrument` and returns it, or None with a printed
    reason. `name` is the dgpy module name: a retry after a failure that got as
    far as registering needs a different one.
    """
    global ec_instrument
    if EC_INSTRUMENT == "none":
        print("  no eddy-current instrument (EC_INSTRUMENT='none'); nothing to "
              "null")
        return None
    if EC_INSTRUMENT != "uniwest":
        raise ValueError("EC_INSTRUMENT must be 'uniwest' or 'none', not %r"
                         % (EC_INSTRUMENT,))
    port = EC_INSTRUMENT_PORT if port is None else int(port)
    baud = EC_INSTRUMENT_BAUD if baud is None else int(baud)
    try:
        from dgpython_uniwest import UniWest
        ec_instrument = UniWest(name, comPort=port, baudRate=baud)
        print("  eddy-current instrument: Uniwest on port %d at %d baud"
              % (port, baud))
        print("    its settings were not touched; the null is the only command "
              "this config sends. Set it up on its front panel in either order.")
    except Exception as _e:                                        # noqa: BLE001
        # Not fatal at load: planning, reconstruction and a dry run need no
        # instrument. run_ec_scan() is where the absence is decided, against
        # EC_NULL_REQUIRED, before anything moves.
        ec_instrument = None
        print("  NO eddy-current instrument on port %d (%s: %s)"
              % (port, type(_e).__name__, _e))
        print("  the scan cannot null on the part; run_ec_scan() %s."
              % ("will REFUSE (EC_NULL_REQUIRED)" if EC_NULL_REQUIRED
                 else "will warn and go ahead un-nulled"))
    return ec_instrument


if EC_INSTRUMENT != "none":
    connect_ec_instrument()


global null_ec_instrument
def null_ec_instrument():
    """Balance the instrument's bridge. THE PROBE MUST BE ON THE PART.

    The hardware null, not the software one: /ec/cond/null (null_here()) is a
    complex offset subtracted from recorded traces and it does not move the
    instrument's operating point.

    The scan calls this by itself at the first touchdown. Called by hand it is
    the manual step from ClientScannerTest ("Null Uniwest?") -- put the probe on
    good material first with ec_touch_down(0).
    """
    if ec_instrument is None and ec_model is not None:
        # The SIMULATED instrument's bridge. Same zero-argument call and the
        # same one correct moment -- probe on the part, before any trace is
        # read -- so the whole nulling sequence is exercised in simulation
        # rather than only on the arm. It can be nulled in mid-air here too,
        # and reports that it was, because that is the bench mistake and
        # catching it in simulation is the point.
        out = ec_model.null()
        print("  simulated instrument nulled at %.4f%+.4fj ohms "
              "(standoff %s, %s)"
              % (out["ohms"].real, out["ohms"].imag,
                 "unknown" if out["standoff_m"] is None
                 else "%.3f mm" % (out["standoff_m"]*1e3,),
                 "OFF THE PART -- this null is wrong" if out["off_part"]
                 else "on the part"))
        return
    if ec_instrument is None:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "there is no eddy-current instrument to null (EC_INSTRUMENT=%r). "
            "connect_ec_instrument() opens one; set_ec_nulling(enabled=False) "
            "tells the scan not to try." % (EC_INSTRUMENT,))
    ec_instrument.null()


global set_ec_nulling
def set_ec_nulling(enabled=None, required=None):
    """Turn the first-touchdown instrument null on or off, and say what a failure
    means. The one way to change either from the prompt.

        set_ec_nulling(enabled=False)     bench work: do not null at all
        set_ec_nulling(required=False)    try, but scan anyway if it fails

    `enabled` is EC_NULL_ON_CONTACT, `required` is EC_NULL_REQUIRED. Passing
    neither just prints the state. A bare assignment to either name at the prompt
    does not reach the shared globals, which is why this exists.
    """
    global EC_NULL_ON_CONTACT, EC_NULL_REQUIRED
    if enabled is not None:
        EC_NULL_ON_CONTACT = bool(enabled)
    if required is not None:
        EC_NULL_REQUIRED = bool(required)
    print(describe_ec_nulling())
    return {"enabled": EC_NULL_ON_CONTACT, "required": EC_NULL_REQUIRED}


global describe_ec_nulling
def describe_ec_nulling():
    """One line stating what the scan will do about the instrument null."""
    if not EC_NULL_ON_CONTACT:
        return ("  instrument null: OFF (set_ec_nulling(enabled=True) to turn it "
                "on). Every trace will carry whatever balance the instrument "
                "happens to be in.")
    if ec_instrument is None:
        return ("  instrument null: requested but there is NO INSTRUMENT "
                "(EC_INSTRUMENT=%r) -- the scan will %s"
                % (EC_INSTRUMENT,
                   "REFUSE to start (EC_NULL_REQUIRED)" if EC_NULL_REQUIRED
                   else "warn and scan un-nulled"))
    return ("  instrument null: at the first touchdown, on the part, before any "
            "trace; a failure %s"
            % ("stops the scan at the first pass" if EC_NULL_REQUIRED
               else "is reported and the scan goes on"))


global ec_ready
def ec_ready():
    """Whether an eddy-current scan can actually run, and why not if it cannot."""
    if daq is None:
        return (False, "no DAQ: EC_SOURCE=%r and the card did not open" % (EC_SOURCE,))
    for path in (EC_REAL_PATH, EC_IMAG_PATH):
        try:
            recdb.latest.ref[path]
        except Exception:
            return (False, "the DAQ is open but %s has no data yet" % (path,))
    return (True, "ready")


global setup_ec
def setup_ec(pitch_m=None, null=None, rotation_deg=None, gain=None):
    """Build the trace source and hold the conditioning parameters. Idempotent.

    THE CONDITIONING IS NOT WIRED HERE, and it cannot be. Its input is the trace
    list, which is an OUTPUT of nd_accumulate_once and does not exist until the
    scan has staged its first reading -- so the scan wires it, in
    run_ec_path_scan()'s setup_after_first hook. This call records null,
    rotation and gain so the values are in force the moment it does.

    The ordering matters: naming a channel that does not exist yet makes
    spatialnde2 throw on the timed transaction manager's own thread, which is
    SIGABRT for the whole process rather than an error anybody can read. See the
    note in ec_acquisition.dpi.

    Still worth calling before a scan: it opens the DAQ path and reports the
    smear, and set_rotation()/null_here() are callable either side of it.
    """
    global ec_source
    ok, why = ec_ready()
    if not ok:
        raise RuntimeError("cannot set up eddy current: %s" % (why,))

    # DOES THE COMPILED CONDITIONING ACTUALLY RUN? Asked here, before anything
    # moves, because the answer can be "it segfaults" and a segfault twenty
    # minutes into a scan with the probe on the part is the worst place to find
    # out. An extension built against a different spatialnde2 imports fine and
    # crashes the process the first time the math executes. This check runs in a
    # child, so the crash is a message. See check_ec_conditioning() in
    # ec_acquisition.dpi.
    cond_ok, cond_why = check_ec_conditioning()
    if not cond_ok:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "the eddy-current conditioning is not usable, so a scan would lose "
            "its data or the whole process:\n  %s" % (cond_why,))

    pitch = SCAN_STEP_SIZE if pitch_m is None else pitch_m
    if ec_source is None:
        ec_source = ECTimestampedSource(
            recdb, transaction_clock, EC_REAL_PATH, EC_IMAG_PATH,
            probe_position, pitch, reduce=EC_BLOCK_REDUCE,
            pose_path="/ec/trigger_pose")
        print("  eddy-current source: one complex sample per %.3f mm, "
              "correlated by timestamp, block reduce=%s"
              % (pitch*1e3, EC_BLOCK_REDUCE))

    held = ec_conditioning_parameters(
        null=EC_NULL if null is None else null,
        rotation_deg=(EC_ROTATION_DEG if rotation_deg is None else rotation_deg),
        gain=EC_GAIN if gain is None else gain,
        cond=ec_cond)
    if ec_cond is None:
        print("  conditioning parameters held for %s -> %s/{conditioned,"
              "horizontal,vertical,magnitude}: null (%.5f, %.5f), rotation "
              "%.1f deg, gain h=%.2f v=%.2f"
              % ((EC_TRACES_PATH, EC_COND_PREFIX) + tuple(held["null"])
                 + (held["rotation"],) + tuple(held["gain"])))
        print("    the math is wired when the scan stages its first trace -- "
              "%s does not exist before that" % (EC_TRACES_PATH,))
    # The HARDWARE balance, which those parameters sit on top of. Said here so
    # the two are never mistaken for one setting.
    print(describe_ec_nulling())
    print(describe_ec_preload())
    return held


global teardown_ec
def teardown_ec():
    """Release the conditioning channels. Required before a second setup_ec().

    define_channel() does not raise when a channel already exists; it hands back
    a reserved_channel that is no longer the current owner, and the NEXT
    transaction fails with 'reserved_channel is not current' nowhere near here.
    """
    global ec_source, ec_cond
    if ec_cond is not None:
        teardown_ec_conditioning(recdb, ec_cond)
        ec_cond = None
    if ec_source is not None:
        ec_source.stop()
        ec_source = None


global null_here
def null_here(count=20):
    """Set the CONDITIONING null from traces just acquired over good material.

    A software offset -- /ec/cond/null, subtracted from every recorded trace by
    the conditioning math and re-runnable over traces already taken. NOT the
    instrument null, which is a hardware balance of the instrument's own bridge,
    issued once with the probe on the part (null_ec_instrument(), and the scan
    does it at the first touchdown). This sits on top of that one; it cannot
    stand in for it, because an instrument balanced in air has its operating
    point in the wrong place before any trace exists.

    Taking the value from traces the operator watched being taken beats typing in
    a number from a previous session on a different probe at a different
    temperature.

    NEEDS TRACES, so it needs a scan (or at least a pass) to have run. There is
    nothing to average before that, which is why it cannot be part of setup.
    """
    if not ec_channel_exists(recdb, EC_TRACES_PATH):
        raise RuntimeError(
            "there are no traces to null on yet: %s does not exist until a scan "
            "has staged its first reading. Run a pass (run_ec_scan(max_passes=1)) "
            "and null on that, or set a value directly with "
            "setup_ec(null=(re, im))." % (EC_TRACES_PATH,))
    null = ec_null_from_traces(recdb, EC_TRACES_PATH, first=0, count=count)
    ec_conditioning_parameters(null=null, cond=ec_cond)
    print("  conditioning null set to (%.5f, %.5f) from the first %d traces%s"
          % (null[0], null[1], count,
             "" if ec_cond is not None else " (held until the scan wires it)"))
    return null


global rotation_from_scan
def rotation_from_scan(first=0, count=None, apply=True, reproject=True,
                       min_traces=200):
    """MEASURE the lift-off rotation from the traces just acquired, and use it.

    THE SETUP PROCEDURE, done from the data instead of by eye. Over good
    material the reading moves almost entirely along one direction in the
    impedance plane -- lift-off and tilt dominate and they share an axis -- so
    the first principal direction of the nulled traces IS the lift-off
    direction, and turning it onto horizontal leaves the part on vertical.

    WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN IT SOUNDS. The projection paints
    /ec/cond/vertical (ec_scan_component). With the rotation unset that channel
    is just the raw imaginary part, so the image is the instrument's standing
    offset plus tilt, and the flaws are a few per cent of it. Measured on a
    2212-trace scan over four notches of 1.016 to 2.540 mm:

        unrotated (magnitude)   noise p99 0.0262 V   SNR 0.6 / 0.9 / 1.5 / 2.6
        rotated   (vertical)    noise p99 0.0023 V   SNR 2.2 / 1.6 / 3.6 / 6.1

    An order of magnitude in the noise floor, and every notch over the floor
    instead of two under it. It costs one call.

    `apply` sets the rotation; `reproject` then re-paints the surface from
    every trace already taken, because the projector paints each reading as it
    arrives and pixels already laid down do not change when the parameters do.
    Pass reproject=False to look at the number without touching the image.

    Returns (degrees, detail). detail["anisotropy"] says how clearly there was
    a direction to find; see ec_liftoff_rotation_from_traces(), which refuses
    rather than inventing an angle from a round scatter.
    """
    if not ec_channel_exists(recdb, EC_TRACES_PATH):
        raise RuntimeError(
            "there are no traces to measure a rotation from: %s does not exist "
            "until a scan has staged its first reading. Run a pass "
            "(run_ec_scan(max_passes=1)) first." % (EC_TRACES_PATH,))
    degrees, detail = ec_liftoff_rotation_from_traces(
        recdb, EC_TRACES_PATH, first=first, count=count, min_traces=min_traces)
    print("  lift-off lies at %+.2f deg over %d trace(s); the scatter is %.1fx "
          "longer along it than across (%.5f vs %.5f V)"
          % (degrees, detail["traces"], detail["anisotropy"],
             detail["sigma_along"], detail["sigma_across"]))
    if not apply:
        print("    not applied (apply=False)")
        return degrees, detail
    set_rotation(degrees)
    if reproject:
        # The pixels already painted carry the OLD rotation. Re-painting from
        # trace 0 is what makes the image agree with the parameters; without it
        # the numbers are right and the picture is still the old one.
        out = project_ec_traces(first=0, count=None)
        print("    re-projected %s trace(s) onto the surface"
              % (out.get("projected") if isinstance(out, dict) else "all",))
    return degrees, detail


global set_rotation
def set_rotation(degrees):
    """Rotate the impedance plane. THIS IS THE SETUP PROCEDURE.

    Turn it until lift-off lies along the horizontal axis; whatever is left
    vertically is the part. Once the math is wired this re-runs over every trace
    already acquired, so it is done against a recorded pass rather than by
    re-scanning. Called before the first scan it sets the value the scan starts
    with.
    """
    val = ec_conditioning_parameters(rotation_deg=float(degrees), cond=ec_cond)
    print("  rotation %.1f degrees%s"
          % (val["rotation"],
             "" if val["live"] else " (held until the scan wires it)"))
    return val["rotation"]


global set_gain
def set_gain(horizontal=1.0, vertical=1.0):
    """Per-axis gain. After rotation the two axes carry different quantities."""
    val = ec_conditioning_parameters(gain=(horizontal, vertical), cond=ec_cond)
    print("  gain h=%.2f v=%.2f%s"
          % (val["gain"][0], val["gain"][1],
             "" if val["live"] else " (held until the scan wires it)"))
    return val["gain"]


global probe_position
def probe_position():
    """Where the probe is now, from the TF holder rather than from the module.

    Returns None before TF has produced anything -- the trigger treats that as
    'no reading' rather than as the origin, which is the difference between
    waiting and silently scanning from the wrong place.
    """
    return live_pose.xyz()

# ==============================================================================
# THE WORKFLOW, ONE STAGE PER FUNCTION
# ==============================================================================
# Each stage takes explicit inputs, returns its result, and can be re-run on its
# own, so a step that fails can be retried without redoing the steps before it.
# No stage asks a question, so the whole workflow is driveable from anything,
# not only from a human at a terminal.
#
# Every stage also leaves its result in a global, so the next one can be called
# from the prompt with no arguments.

global detected_region
global rough_mesh
global fine_mesh
global ec_scan_path
global ec_scan_x3d
detected_region = None
rough_mesh = None
fine_mesh = None
ec_scan_path = None
ec_scan_x3d = None


global detected_info
detected_info = None


global _ec_contact
def _ec_contact():
    """The contact-motion settings, from this config's names."""
    # links= names what may touch the part. run_surface_path_scan() declares it
    # to the planner before the first pass, and without it every descent onto the
    # surface is truncated by the collision checker and every pass is skipped --
    # 0 traces of 720 expected, measured in simulation.
    return contact_motion(lift_m=EC_LIFT_M, approach_m=EC_APPROACH_M,
                          short_hop_m=EC_SHORT_HOP_M,
                          near_speed_mps=NEAR_PART_SPEED,
                          transit_joint_scaling=JOINT_MOVE_SCALING,
                          accel=RASTER_ACCEL, axis_speed_mps=EC_AXIS_SPEED,
                          links=TOOLING_CONTACT_LINKS)


global _check_contact_speed
def _check_contact_speed():
    if SCAN_VELOCITY > NEAR_PART_SPEED*(1.0 + 1e-9):
        raise RuntimeError(
            "SCAN_VELOCITY is %.1f mm/s, and the probe is in contact with the part "
            "for the whole sweep. The limit near the part is %.1f mm/s "
            "(NEAR_PART_SPEED). Lower SCAN_VELOCITY: "
            "globals()['SCAN_VELOCITY'] = %.3f"
            % (SCAN_VELOCITY*1e3, NEAR_PART_SPEED*1e3, NEAR_PART_SPEED))


global move_to_named_pose
def move_to_named_pose(name):
    """Joint move to a named state in the cell's SRDF. Raises if refused.

    Holds the warning light itself, so it is on even when this is called from
    the prompt rather than from a stage (holds nest; see warning_light()).
    """
    with warning_light("moving to %s" % (name,)):
        r = ros.JointMove(name, **joint_move_kwargs())
    if not bool(getattr(getattr(r, "result", None), "success", False)):
        raise RuntimeError(
            "the arm would not move to %r: MoveIt refused it (mtc_node's log says "
            "why). From an invalid start state every goal is refused -- check with "
            "ros2 service call /check_state_validity moveit_msgs/srv/GetStateValidity "
            "\"{group_name: ur_manipulator}\"" % (name,))
    return r


global park_arm
def park_arm(pose=None):
    """Rise straight up out of the work volume, then go to PARK_POSE.

    Straight up first, because a joint move from near the part sweeps the tool
    wherever the arm's geometry takes it and the part is not in MoveIt's
    planning scene. The first EC_APPROACH_M at the near-part speed, the rest at
    TRANSIT_SPEED, up to the work volume's ceiling.

    A probe still on the part -- ec_touch_down() without ec_lift() -- is first
    backed off along its OWN axis, as every contact move is; rising in world z
    from a tilted surface would drag the tip across it.

    The rise is held in the `transit` motion profile rather than taking whatever
    the session left in force: it has to COMPLETE, and a tenth of a millimetre of
    shortfall at the work volume's ceiling costs nothing. See the comment on it.
    """
    pose = PARK_POSE if pose is None else pose
    with warning_light("parking the arm"):
        if ec_scan_path is not None:
            with motion_profile("scan"):
                contact_retract(_ec_contact(), positions=ec_scan_path.positions)
        snap = live_pose.snapshot()
        if snap is not None:
            p = np.asarray(snap[0], dtype=np.float64)
            q = tuple(float(v) for v in snap[1])
            vol = work_volume()
            top = vol[2][2] if vol is not None else p[2] + EC_APPROACH_M
            z = float(p[2])
            # `transit`, and NAMED rather than inherited. The rise is a clearance
            # move: it has to COMPLETE (min_fraction=1.0 below -- a short rise is
            # not a park) and it does not matter within a millimetre where it
            # stops, since the target is the top of the work volume. That is
            # exactly what transit's 0.1 mm position_threshold is for; scan's
            # 0.01 mm sometimes loses a multi-waypoint Cartesian path instead
            # (measured: 5 of 5 rows completed at 1e-4, 1 of 5 refused partway
            # at 1e-5).
            #
            # Naming it matters because a park runs when something has ALREADY
            # gone wrong, from wherever the failure left the arm; inheriting
            # whatever profile the session happens to hold -- `scan`, if a stage
            # above it is still inside a hold -- gives a recovery path different
            # settings every time.
            #
            # Not about IK branch: the rise is flown by mtc_hub_node, which is
            # `mode: local` in both profiles, so it follows its seed and stays in
            # the arm's configuration either way. (The cable guard asks in the
            # mode the executing node holds -- see CABLE_GUARD_EXECUTORS in
            # cell_capture_motion.dpi -- so it does not need a profile here to
            # be right either.)
            with motion_profile("transit"):
                for z_to, speed in ((min(z + EC_APPROACH_M, top), NEAR_PART_SPEED),
                                    (top, TRANSIT_SPEED)):
                    if z_to <= z + 0.001:
                        continue
                    r = ros.CartesianMove([_pose_msg((p[0], p[1], z), q),
                                           _pose_msg((p[0], p[1], z_to), q)],
                                          vel=motion_scaling(speed), acc=RASTER_ACCEL,
                                          min_fraction=1.0)
                    if not bool(getattr(getattr(r, "result", None), "success", False)):
                        print("  could not rise straight up to z %.3f; parking from z %.3f"
                              % (z_to, z))
                        break
                    z = z_to
        if pose:
            move_to_named_pose(pose)
            print("  parked at %s" % (pose,))


global detect_part
def detect_part(index=0, pad=0.0, move=True, **kwargs):
    """Find the specimen with YOLO and return the region to measure.

    Not a hard dependency of anything downstream: every stage takes `region`
    explicitly, so a specimen the detector cannot see is set by hand and nothing
    else changes -- run_automated_scan(region=(xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax)).

    move=True goes to DETECT_POSE first: a detection is only as good as the
    view, and detecting from wherever a sweep left the arm clips the part at the
    edge of the image.
    """
    global detected_region, detected_info
    if move and DETECT_POSE:
        with warning_light("moving to %s to look for the part" % (DETECT_POSE,)):
            move_to_named_pose(DETECT_POSE)
    # yolo_region() returns (region, info); the region alone is what the Azure
    # sweep and every later stage take.
    detected_region, detected_info = yolo_region(index=index, pad=pad, **kwargs)
    print("  detected region: x %.3f..%.3f  y %.3f..%.3f  (%s, score %.2f, "
          "surface z %.3f)"
          % (detected_region[0], detected_region[2], detected_region[1],
             detected_region[3], detected_info.get("class_name"),
             detected_info.get("score", 0.0), detected_info.get("surface_z", 0.0)))
    return detected_region


global _resolve_azure_sweep
def _resolve_azure_sweep(kwargs, region=None):
    """Turn AZURE_ORIENTATION into the sweep kwargs, PRE-CHECKING the orbit.

    Mutates `kwargs` in place and returns the mode actually resolved to, which
    is not always the one asked for: see below.

    THE THREE MODES, and what each one costs the arm:

      "z"       style stays whatever SPECIMEN_SWEEP_STYLE says (dome here) and
                rot=False. ONE orientation for every stop -- whatever the arm is
                already standing in, so it is inside the cable band by
                construction -- and the parallax comes from translation alone.
                At 530 mm NFOV_UNBINNED sees 813 x 675 mm against a 135 x 286 mm
                part, so every stop already sees the whole part and the raster is
                redundant views rather than coverage. That is what lets
                _plan_dome() SHRINK the grid to fit the reach instead of refusing.
      "normal"  the same dome with rot=True: domePoseArray's own +/-15 deg sweep
                of the camera about the surface normal, so the TSDF gets more
                than one ray per point and can close a little relief instead of
                integrating a pure height field. It costs wrist travel -- the
                tilt is applied to the base orientation the arm is holding -- and
                _dome_reachable_grid() already checks every stop against the
                measured band and drops or refuses accordingly.
      "orbit"   style="pan": 8 candidate azimuths, and to face each one the
                camera is ROLLED to it, which is wrist_3.

    WHY THE ORBIT WAS TAKEN OUT, because re-enabling it without the pre-check
    would walk straight back into it. The pan sweep buys views of the SIDES of
    the part, and the sides of a thin flat plate cannot be reached from this cell
    at all -- so the whole cost was paid for something that was never collected.
    Worse, it is FRAGILE IN A SESSION: the same region can be admissible at plan
    time and refused at all 8 azimuths and all 6 heights minutes later, and that
    refusal takes out every stage downstream because nothing gets a rough mesh.
    results16 shows the IK health going 25 of 25 -> 24 of 25 -> 17 of 25 stops
    reachable over one afternoon, over the same bench, which is exactly the drift
    that makes "it planned an hour ago" worth nothing.

    SO THE ORBIT IS PRE-CHECKED THROUGH THE SAME PLANNER IT WILL FLY, with
    plan_only=True, before the recorder is started or the arm is moved. A refusal
    is reported in full and the stage falls back to the locked-Z dome
    (AZURE_ORBIT_REQUIRED=True refuses instead). The check is a plan, not a
    promise -- IK is randomised and the drift above is real -- but it converts
    "dies mid-sweep with a half-written TSDF" into "says so and flies the sweep
    that works".
    """
    mode = check_sensor_orientation("azure", AZURE_ORIENTATION)
    if mode in ("z", "normal"):
        kwargs.setdefault("rot", mode == "normal")
        return mode
    # "orbit"
    if kwargs.get("plan_only"):
        # THIS CALL IS the check -- check_rough_plan() -- so running another one
        # inside it would plan the same sweep twice and report the first.
        kwargs.setdefault("style", "pan")
        return mode
    probe = dict(kwargs)
    probe.pop("rot", None)
    probe["style"] = "pan"
    probe["plan_only"] = True
    print("    orbit requested (set_sensor_orientation(azure=...)): planning it "
          "first, through the same planner it would fly. Nothing moves.")
    try:
        plan = reconstruct_specimen(source="azure", region=region,
                                    name="specimen", **probe)
    except Exception as e:                                    # noqa: BLE001
        why = (str(e).splitlines() or [repr(e)])[0]
        if AZURE_ORBIT_REQUIRED:
            raise RuntimeError(
                "the orbiting Azure sweep cannot be flown over this part and "
                "AZURE_ORBIT_REQUIRED is True, so the stage is refused rather "
                "than quietly flown straight down: %s" % (why,))
        print("    THE PART CANNOT BE ORBITED: %s" % (why,))
        print("    falling back to the locked-Z dome, which is what this cell "
              "has always flown. Nothing has moved. set "
              "AZURE_ORBIT_REQUIRED=True to have this refuse the stage instead, "
              "or set_sensor_orientation(azure='z') to stop asking.")
        kwargs.setdefault("rot", False)
        return "z"
    print("    orbit pre-check passed: %s" % (plan.describe(),))
    kwargs.setdefault("style", "pan")
    return mode


global reconstruct_rough
def reconstruct_rough(region=None, purpose="planning", **kwargs):
    """Azure sweep into a TSDF. A PLANNING SURFACE, not a measurement.

    Coarse on purpose: specimen.dpi defaults the voxel to 0.006 rather than
    0.002 because at 0.002 the TSDF resolves every hole and noise bump and
    Noether then follows all of it -- 8 m of raster "lines" on a 0.35 m part,
    measured. 0.006 gives eleven sane segments.

    purpose="planning" IS THE POINT OF THIS STAGE, and it is declared here
    rather than left to a global default because this function is the one
    caller that can honestly say it. What comes out of here is handed to
    reconstruct_fine() to plan a Keyence raster over and to Noether to plan an
    eddy-current toolpath over -- both of which want the TOP of the part and
    neither of which can use its sides. A height field is that surface. The
    global minimum (AZURE_PAN_MIN_DIRECTIONS = 2) is right for a sweep whose
    output is the measurement, and holding this stage to it refuses the whole
    run whenever only one direction is admissible. Pass purpose="closed" to hold
    it to the stricter rule.
    """
    global rough_mesh
    region = detected_region if region is None else region
    kwargs.setdefault("purpose", purpose)
    # A STRAIGHT-DOWN RASTER AT ONE FIXED ORIENTATION, which is the whole of
    # what this stage needs and is the cheapest thing the arm can be asked for.
    #
    # The pan sweep orbits the part: 8 candidate azimuths, and to face each one
    # the camera has to be ROLLED to it, which is wrist_3. That buys views of
    # the SIDES of the part -- and the sides of a thin flat plate cannot be
    # reached from this cell at all, so the whole cost is paid for something
    # that is never collected. It is also fragile: the same region can be
    # admissible at plan time and then refused at all 8 azimuths and all 6
    # heights minutes later, which takes out every stage downstream.
    #
    # rot=False holds ONE orientation for every stop: whatever the arm is
    # already in, which is in the cable band by construction because the arm is
    # standing in it. The stops then differ only in x and y, and the parallax
    # comes from translation. At 530 mm NFOV_UNBINNED sees 813 x 675 mm, so
    # every stop sees the entire part; the raster is redundant views, not
    # coverage, and it can be shrunk to fit the reach without losing any of the
    # part. That is what _plan_dome() now does instead of refusing.
    #
    # set_sensor_orientation(azure=...) IS HOW THE ORBIT COMES BACK, and it is
    # resolved here rather than at SPECIMEN_SWEEP_STYLE because the orbit needs a
    # pre-check that only this stage can run. See _resolve_azure_sweep().
    # NOT kwargs.setdefault("style", "dome"): the style is declared once, at
    # SPECIMEN_SWEEP_STYLE, where check_sweep_plan() and capture_full_pipeline()
    # can both see it. Forcing it here makes this stage fly a sweep the config
    # does not describe. Pass style= or rot= to override for one call.
    _resolve_azure_sweep(kwargs, region=region)
    if kwargs.get("plan_only"):
        # NOT into rough_mesh: a plan is a ScanPath, and parking one there
        # would have reconstruct_fine() believe stage 2 had produced a surface.
        # Nothing moves, so no warning light either.
        return reconstruct_specimen(source="azure", region=region,
                                    name="specimen", **kwargs)
    with warning_light("Azure sweep (rough planning surface)"):
        rough_mesh = reconstruct_specimen(source="azure", region=region,
                                          name="specimen", **kwargs)
    print("  rough mesh: %s" % (rough_mesh,))
    return rough_mesh


global check_rough_plan
def check_rough_plan(region=None, **kwargs):
    """WILL STAGE 2 FLY? The same plan reconstruct_rough() would make. No motion.

    It is reconstruct_rough() with plan_only=True, so it goes through the same
    source, the same kwargs and the same planner -- which is the point. A
    checker that is a separate implementation of the thing it checks will
    eventually disagree with it, and IK is randomised, so "eventually" is every
    run: check_sweep_plan() has reported a sweep flying from 2 directions where
    reconstruct_rough(), over the same region minutes later, found 0 of 8 and
    refused.

    Run it before the recorder starts. It prints the raster, where probe_end
    ends up, the standoff against the depth mode, and how many stops reach.
    """
    region = detected_region if region is None else region
    return reconstruct_rough(region=region, plan_only=True, **kwargs)


global reconstruct_fine
def reconstruct_fine(rough=None, region=None, **kwargs):
    """Keyence profile raster over the rough mesh: the measurement.

    Needs a surface to plan its raster over, which is what the Azure pass is
    for. So for a part that has never been measured the honest order is
    reconstruct_rough() then reconstruct_fine(), which is what
    run_automated_scan() does. orient= defaults to KEYENCE_ORIENTATION.
    """
    global fine_mesh
    rough = rough_mesh if rough is None else rough
    if rough is None:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "the Keyence raster needs a surface to plan over. Run "
            "reconstruct_rough() first, or pass rough= a mesh on disk.")
    kwargs.setdefault("orient", KEYENCE_ORIENTATION)
    with warning_light("Keyence raster"):
        fine_mesh = reconstruct_specimen(source="keyence", rough_mesh=rough,
                                         region=detected_region if region is None else region,
                                         name="specimen", **kwargs)
    print("  fine mesh: %s" % (fine_mesh,))
    return fine_mesh


global filter_mesh
def filter_mesh(mesh=None, cutoff=None, **kwargs):
    """Run tari_mesh_filter over a reconstructed mesh.

    Two things about the service make it easy to call and get a silent no-op.
    It runs ONE filter per call (if color_filter / elif vert_filter / elif
    delete_loose_parts), so asking for two gets the first; and `cutoff` is a
    plane in WORLD z, which removes nothing at all unless it is set for a cell
    whose bench sits at z 0.92-1.06.

    cutoff=None means do not run the vertex filter at all, which is what lets
    loose-part removal run. Set MESH_CUTOFF_Z for this cell to use it.
    """
    mesh = (fine_mesh or rough_mesh) if mesh is None else mesh
    if mesh is None:
        raise RuntimeError("nothing to filter -- reconstruct something first")
    cutoff = MESH_CUTOFF_Z if cutoff is None else cutoff
    out = clean_mesh(mesh, cutoff=cutoff, **kwargs)
    print("  filtered mesh: %s" % (out,))
    return out


global ec_clearance_report
ec_clearance_report = None

#: What the last plan_ec_path() found and did about poses whose tool axis did not
#: agree with the surface: see repair_tool_axis_to_surface().
global ec_axis_flip_report
ec_axis_flip_report = None


# ==============================================================================
# THE CONTACT PRELOAD
# ==============================================================================
# One gate, one setter, one line of output. See EC_PRELOAD_M above for what the
# number is and EC_PRELOAD_CEILING_M for where the ceiling comes from.

global check_ec_preload
def check_ec_preload(metres):
    """The preload, validated, in metres. Raises ValueError saying why not.

    ONE GATE FOR BOTH WAYS IN -- set_ec_preload() from the prompt and
    plan_ec_path(preload=) from a script -- so neither can command a push the
    other would have refused.
    """
    try:
        value = float(metres)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        raise ValueError("the preload is a distance in metres, not %r" % (metres,))
    if not np.isfinite(value):
        raise ValueError("the preload is a distance in metres, not %r" % (metres,))
    if value < 0.0:
        raise ValueError(
            "a preload of %.3f mm is NEGATIVE, which is lift-off and not "
            "preload: it commands the tip that far ABOVE the measured surface "
            "and the probe leaves the part. The allowed range is 0 to %.2f mm "
            "(EC_PRELOAD_CEILING_M)."
            % (value*1e3, EC_PRELOAD_CEILING_M*1e3))
    if value > EC_PRELOAD_CEILING_M:
        raise ValueError(
            "a preload of %.2f mm is over the %.2f mm ceiling "
            "(EC_PRELOAD_CEILING_M). The fixture has 4.5 mm of plunger inside "
            "its bearing tube and the probe mesh ends 0.25 mm past the "
            "commanded tip, so past the ceiling the arm presses on the part "
            "instead of on the spring: the spring bottoms out, the reading "
            "saturates, and the probe or the specimen is what gives. For "
            "margin, shrink the scan area -- never press harder."
            % (value*1e3, EC_PRELOAD_CEILING_M*1e3))
    return value


global set_ec_preload
def set_ec_preload(metres):
    """Set the contact preload, IN METRES. The one way to change it.

        set_ec_preload(0.0003)    0.30 mm past the measured surface
        set_ec_preload(0)         the tip exactly on the measured surface

    Metres, not millimetres -- see EC_PRELOAD_M. Refused below 0 (that is
    lift-off) and above EC_PRELOAD_CEILING_M.

    It takes effect at the NEXT plan_ec_path(). A toolpath already planned
    carries the preload it was planned with, and run_ec_scan() prints that one
    rather than this setting, so re-plan after changing it.
    """
    global EC_PRELOAD_M
    EC_PRELOAD_M = check_ec_preload(metres)
    print(describe_ec_preload())
    return EC_PRELOAD_M


global ec_preload
def ec_preload():
    """The preload in force now, in metres. Prints it in millimetres."""
    print(describe_ec_preload())
    return EC_PRELOAD_M


global describe_ec_preload
def describe_ec_preload(preload=None):
    """One line stating the preload in millimetres. Never blank, zero included.

    `preload` None means the current setting; pass a path's own recorded value to
    say what that path was planned with.
    """
    value = EC_PRELOAD_M if preload is None else float(preload)
    if value > 0.0:
        return ("  preload: %.2f mm past the measured surface at every pose, "
                "along the tool axis (set_ec_preload, ceiling %.2f mm)"
                % (value*1e3, EC_PRELOAD_CEILING_M*1e3))
    return ("  preload: 0.00 mm -- the tip is commanded exactly onto the "
            "measured surface, so mesh error becomes lift-off or compression "
            "1:1. set_ec_preload(metres) presses the probe into the part.")


global ec_probe_rotation_reference
def ec_probe_rotation_reference(degrees=None):
    """The roll reference with the probe turned by EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG.

    ONE PLACE BUILDS IT, so plan_ec_path() and every report agree about what the
    probe is holding. Returns a reference ORIENTATION as (x, y, z, w), which is
    what every roll-aware function takes in place of a direction.
    """
    deg = EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG if degrees is None else float(degrees)
    return probe_rotation_reference(deg, NOETHER_ROLL_REFERENCE)


global check_ec_probe_rotation
def check_ec_probe_rotation(degrees, path=None, verbose=True):
    """Validate a probe rotation against the measured cable band. -> the report.

    ONE GATE FOR BOTH WAYS IN -- set_probe_rotation() from the prompt and
    plan_ec_path(probe_rotation_deg=) from a script -- so neither can command a
    rotation the other would have refused.
    """
    try:
        value = float(degrees)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        raise ValueError("a probe rotation is an angle in degrees, not %r"
                         % (degrees,))
    if not np.isfinite(value):
        raise ValueError("a probe rotation is an angle in degrees, not %r"
                         % (degrees,))
    if abs(value) > 360.0:
        raise ValueError(
            "a probe rotation of %+.1f deg is more than a full turn. The split of "
            "a split-D coil is a LINE, so every distinct orientation is inside "
            "-180..+180 -- and the cables care about the absolute wrist angle, so "
            "a larger number is a different cable state rather than the same "
            "measurement." % (value,))
    return check_probe_rotation(
        value, path=path, datum_deg=EC_PROBE_ROTATION_WRIST3_DEG,
        reference=NOETHER_ROLL_REFERENCE, what="probe rotation", verbose=verbose)


global set_probe_rotation
def set_probe_rotation(degrees):
    """Turn the eddy-current probe about its own axis, in DEGREES. Held for the
    whole scan.

        set_probe_rotation(0)      the split along world +x over the part
        set_probe_rotation(90)     the split across it

    A split-D coil answers to cracks PERPENDICULAR to its split and is nearly
    blind to ones parallel to it, so this is which flaw direction the scan is
    looking for. Zero is defined in the part's frame -- see EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG.

    NOT set_rotation(), which is the instrument's impedance phase rotation and
    changes no geometry at all. This one turns the arm's wrist.

    Refused before anything moves when the cables cannot hold it, naming the
    wrist_3 angle it implies and the measured band. It takes effect at the NEXT
    plan_ec_path(); a toolpath already planned carries the rotation it was
    planned with.
    """
    global EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG
    check_ec_probe_rotation(degrees, verbose=False)
    EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG = float(degrees)
    print(describe_probe_rotation())
    return EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG


global probe_rotation
def probe_rotation():
    """The probe rotation in force now, in degrees. Prints it."""
    print(describe_probe_rotation())
    return EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG


global describe_probe_rotation
def describe_probe_rotation(degrees=None):
    """One line stating the probe rotation and what zero means. Never blank.

    `degrees` None means the current setting; pass a path's own recorded value to
    say what that path was planned with.
    """
    value = EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG if degrees is None else float(degrees)
    # The measured datum in preference to the config's, and SAY WHICH. The two can
    # disagree -- the constant is a transcription and the tooling may have been
    # unbolted since -- and an operator reading a wrist angle needs to know
    # whether it came from a measurement of this cell or from a line in a file.
    w3, source = None, ""
    datum_fn = globals().get("wrist3_datum_deg")
    if callable(datum_fn):
        try:
            w3 = datum_fn(tilt=(0.0, 0.0))
        except Exception:                                         # noqa: BLE001
            w3 = None
        if w3 is not None:
            source = ", measured"
    if w3 is None:
        w3 = globals().get("EC_PROBE_ROTATION_WRIST3_DEG")
        if w3 is not None:
            source = ", from the config not a measurement"
    band = ""
    if w3 is not None:
        band = (", wrist 3 at %+.1f deg%s"
                % (probe_rotation_wrist3_deg(value, w3), source))
    return ("  probe rotation: %+.1f deg about the probe's own axis, the same at "
            "every waypoint of every pass. Zero is the split along %s in the "
            "part's frame%s (set_probe_rotation)."
            % (value, tuple(float(c) for c in NOETHER_ROLL_REFERENCE), band))


# ==============================================================================
# SENSOR ORIENTATION -- ONE GATE, ONE SETTER, ONE LINE OF OUTPUT
# ==============================================================================
# The same shape as set_ec_preload() and set_probe_rotation() above, for the same
# reason: one validator both ways in, a setter that prints what it did, and a
# describe_* that the startup banner and the stage both use so they cannot
# disagree. See AZURE_ORIENTATION / KEYENCE_ORIENTATION for the vocabulary.

#: What each sensor will answer to. Per sensor, because "orbit" is a camera's
#: option and means nothing to a line profiler: a profiler measures a LINE at a
#: fixed working distance, and flying it round the part is not a mode, it is a
#: raster with the rows in the wrong place.
global SENSOR_ORIENTATION_MODES
SENSOR_ORIENTATION_MODES = {
    "azure": ("z", "normal", "orbit"),
    # "surface", "capped" and "auto" are the planner's own names, accepted so a
    # script can still ask for the raw normal-following mode with no fallback.
    # They are not offered to an operator: "normal" is the one that degrades
    # safely, and that is the one to reach for.
    "keyence": ("z", "normal", "surface", "capped", "auto"),
}

#: Older and plainer spellings, resolved to the canonical name. "fixed" is what
#: this config has always written for the locked-Z mode and is still what the
#: planner is handed, so it must keep working.
global SENSOR_ORIENTATION_ALIASES
SENSOR_ORIENTATION_ALIASES = {
    "fixed": "z", "world_z": "z", "worldz": "z", "vertical": "z",
    "locked": "z", "down": "z", "dome": "z",
    "specimen": "normal", "part": "normal",
    "pan": "orbit", "orbiting": "orbit",
}


global check_sensor_orientation
def check_sensor_orientation(sensor, mode):
    """The canonical orientation mode for `sensor`, or ValueError saying why not.

    ONE GATE FOR BOTH WAYS IN -- set_sensor_orientation() from the prompt and
    AZURE_ORIENTATION / KEYENCE_ORIENTATION read by a stage -- so neither can
    command a mode the other would have refused.
    """
    which = str(sensor).strip().lower()
    if which not in SENSOR_ORIENTATION_MODES:
        raise ValueError("there is no sensor %r here; this cell has %s"
                         % (sensor, " and ".join(sorted(
                             SENSOR_ORIENTATION_MODES))))
    if mode is None:
        raise ValueError("an orientation mode is one of %s, not None"
                         % (", ".join(SENSOR_ORIENTATION_MODES[which]),))
    want = str(mode).strip().lower().replace("-", "_")
    want = SENSOR_ORIENTATION_ALIASES.get(want, want)
    if want not in SENSOR_ORIENTATION_MODES[which]:
        extra = ""
        if want == "orbit":
            extra = (" Orbiting is a CAMERA's option: it buys views of the "
                     "sides of the part, and a line profiler at a fixed working "
                     "distance has nothing to do with them.")
        raise ValueError(
            "%r is not an orientation the %s can be flown at. It takes %s.%s"
            % (mode, which, ", ".join(SENSOR_ORIENTATION_MODES[which]), extra))
    return want


global set_sensor_orientation
def set_sensor_orientation(azure=None, keyence=None):
    """How the Azure and the Keyence are pointed. Either, both, live.

        set_sensor_orientation(keyence="normal")   follow the part's normal
        set_sensor_orientation(azure="orbit")      fly the part from several sides
        set_sensor_orientation(azure="z", keyence="z")   back to the default

    "z" locks the tool axis to world z, which is what this cell has always
    flown. "normal" follows the specimen's surface. "orbit" is the Azure's pan
    sweep, several azimuths, and is pre-checked before anything moves.

    Refused before anything moves when the mode does not exist for that sensor.
    It takes effect at the NEXT reconstruct_rough() / reconstruct_fine(); a plan
    already in hand carries the orientation it was planned with, and the sweep's
    own "keyence sweep: ... (orient ...)" line reports that one.
    """
    global AZURE_ORIENTATION, KEYENCE_ORIENTATION
    # BOTH VALIDATED BEFORE EITHER IS STORED, so a call naming one good mode and
    # one bad one leaves the pair as it found it rather than half applied.
    pending = {}
    if azure is not None:
        pending["azure"] = check_sensor_orientation("azure", azure)
    if keyence is not None:
        pending["keyence"] = check_sensor_orientation("keyence", keyence)
    if not pending:
        raise ValueError(
            "set_sensor_orientation() needs azure= or keyence= (or both). "
            "sensor_orientation() reads them without changing anything.")
    if "azure" in pending:
        AZURE_ORIENTATION = pending["azure"]
    if "keyence" in pending:
        KEYENCE_ORIENTATION = pending["keyence"]
    print(describe_sensor_orientation())
    return dict(azure=AZURE_ORIENTATION, keyence=KEYENCE_ORIENTATION)


global sensor_orientation
def sensor_orientation():
    """The orientation modes in force now. Prints them."""
    print(describe_sensor_orientation())
    return dict(azure=check_sensor_orientation("azure", AZURE_ORIENTATION),
                keyence=check_sensor_orientation("keyence", KEYENCE_ORIENTATION))


global describe_sensor_orientation
def describe_sensor_orientation(azure=None, keyence=None):
    """Lines stating how each sensor is pointed. Never blank.

    `azure`/`keyence` None mean the current settings; pass a value to say what
    some other run was flown with.
    """
    says = {
        ("azure", "z"): ("LOCKED TO WORLD Z -- the straight-down dome at one "
                         "fixed orientation, 0 deg of wrist travel"),
        ("azure", "normal"): ("NORMAL TO THE SPECIMEN -- the dome, with the "
                              "camera swept about the surface normal so the "
                              "TSDF sees more than one ray per point"),
        ("azure", "orbit"): ("ORBITING -- the pan sweep, several azimuths round "
                             "the part; pre-checked, and it falls back to the "
                             "locked-Z dome if the part cannot be orbited"),
        ("keyence", "z"): ("LOCKED TO WORLD Z -- the laser line across the "
                           "direction of travel, whatever the surface does"),
        ("keyence", "normal"): ("NORMAL TO THE SPECIMEN -- capped at %.0f deg, "
                                "with a per-waypoint fallback to locked-Z "
                                "wherever tilt, cables, clearance or standoff "
                                "refuse it"
                                % (KEYENCE_NORMAL_MAX_TILT_DEG,)),
    }
    lines = ["  sensor orientation (set_sensor_orientation):"]
    for which, value in (("azure", AZURE_ORIENTATION if azure is None else azure),
                         ("keyence", KEYENCE_ORIENTATION if keyence is None
                          else keyence)):
        try:
            mode = check_sensor_orientation(which, value)
        except ValueError as e:
            lines.append("    %-8s %r IS NOT A VALID MODE (%s)"
                         % (which, value, e))
            continue
        lines.append("    %-8s %s"
                     % (which, says.get((which, mode))
                        or "%s -- the planner's own mode, no fallback" % (mode,)))
    # The fallback note only when the Keyence is actually in the normal mode,
    # and never at the cost of raising: this is the startup banner, and a bad
    # constant in a config is precisely when somebody needs to be told rather
    # than handed a traceback instead of the rest of the banner.
    try:
        kmode = check_sensor_orientation(
            "keyence", KEYENCE_ORIENTATION if keyence is None else keyence)
    except ValueError:
        kmode = None
    if kmode == "normal" and not KEYENCE_ORIENT_FALLBACK:
        lines.append("    KEYENCE_ORIENT_FALLBACK is OFF: a waypoint the normal "
                     "pose cannot hold is DROPPED rather than flown vertical")
    return "\n".join(lines)


global say_path_probe_rotation
def say_path_probe_rotation(path):
    """Print the probe rotation THIS toolpath was planned with, and prove it.

    From the path's own poses, not from the setting: a plan built before
    set_probe_rotation() was called still commands the old rotation.
    """
    value = getattr(path, "probe_rotation_deg", None)
    if value is None:
        print("  probe rotation: UNKNOWN -- this toolpath records none, so which "
              "way the coil is turned cannot be stated. Re-plan with "
              "plan_ec_path().")
        return None
    held = probe_rotation_of(path.quats, NOETHER_ROLL_REFERENCE)
    print(describe_probe_rotation(value))
    print("    measured over the %d planned poses: %+.3f..%+.3f deg (spread "
          "%.2e deg). Travel direction does not enter it."
          % (len(held), held.min(), held.max(), held.max() - held.min()))
    return value


global say_path_preload
def say_path_preload(path):
    """Print the preload THIS toolpath was planned with, in millimetres.

    From the path, not from the current setting: a plan built before
    set_ec_preload() was called still commands the old push, and saying
    otherwise would be worse than saying nothing.
    """
    value = getattr(path, "ec_preload_m", None)
    if value is None:
        print("  preload: UNKNOWN -- this toolpath records none, so what the tip "
              "is commanded onto cannot be stated. Re-plan with plan_ec_path().")
        return None
    print(describe_ec_preload(value))
    return float(value)


global plan_ec_path
def plan_ec_path(mesh=None, line_spacing=None, region=None, preload=None,
                 max_tilt_deg=None, axis_smoothing_m=None,
                 probe_rotation_deg=None, force=False, **kwargs):
    """Plan the eddy-current toolpath over a mesh with Noether.

    line_spacing is the distance BETWEEN passes; SCAN_STEP_SIZE is the distance
    between samples ALONG a pass. They are different quantities and only the
    second is a technician control, because the first is a property of the probe
    footprint.

    The tip is planned ON the measured surface (NOETHER_Z_OFFSET 0; probe_end
    is the tip on this tooling), pushed the contact preload further along its
    own axis -- EC_PRELOAD_M, or `preload` here, both through check_ec_preload()
    -- turned about its own axis by the set probe rotation
    (EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG, or `probe_rotation_deg` here, both through
    check_ec_probe_rotation()), locked in roll (NOETHER_TOOL_ROLL), and then
    cropped: to the cell's
    work volume, which a contact probe must never leave -- on a cell that has
    not measured one this refuses -- and to EC_SCAN_REGION if it is set, which
    is how a 0.3 mm scan is kept to an area that fits in the hour the plan
    allows.
    """
    global ec_scan_path, ec_scan_x3d, ec_clearance_report, ec_axis_flip_report
    mesh = (fine_mesh or rough_mesh) if mesh is None else mesh
    region = EC_SCAN_REGION if region is None else region
    # Through the same gate the setter uses, so `preload=` cannot ask for a push
    # set_ec_preload() would have refused.
    preload = check_ec_preload(EC_PRELOAD_M if preload is None else preload)
    # THE PROBE'S OWN ROTATION, REFUSED HERE IF THE CABLES CANNOT HOLD IT -- before
    # the planner is asked for anything and long before the arm moves. The band
    # depends on how far the tool is leaned, so this is the untilted check; the
    # per-pass one runs on the finished path at the bottom of this function, where
    # the tilts the path really needs are known.
    rotation = (EC_PROBE_ROTATION_DEG if probe_rotation_deg is None
                else float(probe_rotation_deg))
    check_ec_probe_rotation(rotation, verbose=False)
    print(describe_probe_rotation(rotation))
    # ONE reference orientation with the rotation baked in. Every pose is that
    # orientation swung onto the surface normal, and a swing adds no rotation
    # about the tool's own axis -- so the rotation is constant over the whole
    # path by construction, in both serpentine directions, and the direction of
    # travel never enters it.
    kwargs.setdefault("roll_reference", ec_probe_rotation_reference(rotation))
    max_tilt = EC_MAX_TILT_DEG if max_tilt_deg is None else max_tilt_deg
    smooth = EC_AXIS_SMOOTHING_M if axis_smoothing_m is None else axis_smoothing_m
    require_work_volume("the eddy-current toolpath")

    # PRICE IT BEFORE BUILDING IT. The planner works at the mesh's resolution
    # rather than the scan's, so a line spacing the technician can type in one
    # second can ask for a million waypoints and hundreds of megabytes of
    # planner reply -- and the failure mode is not a slow plan, it is the dgpy
    # process being OOM-killed (8.4 GB has done it) with every stage before it
    # thrown away. force=True is the deliberate override and still prints what
    # is wrong.
    ec_plan_estimate = ec_plan_cost(region=region, line_spacing=line_spacing,
                                    mesh=mesh, report=True)
    if ec_plan_estimate["plan_errors"] and not force:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "this plan will not fit: %s. Scan a smaller area (EC_SCAN_REGION), "
            "widen SCAN_LINE_SPACING, or plan_ec_path(force=True)."
            % ("; ".join(ec_plan_estimate["plan_errors"]),))

    cr = None
    if EC_CLEAN_PLANNING_MESH and mesh and str(mesh).endswith(".ply"):
        cleaned = os.path.splitext(str(mesh))[0] + "_ecplan.ply"
        cr = clean_mesh_for_planning(str(mesh), cleaned)
        print("  planning mesh %s: %d of %d faces kept (%d on %d non-manifold edges, "
              "%d in %d of %d components under 1%%)"
              % (os.path.basename(cleaned), cr["faces_out"], cr["faces_in"],
                 cr["faces_on_nonmanifold_edges"], cr["nonmanifold_edges"],
                 cr["faces_in_small_components"], cr["components"] - cr["components_kept"],
                 cr["components"]))
        mesh = cleaned
    # CHECK CLEARANCE LAST, NOT FIRST. plan_mesh_path() runs the tooling gate
    # over the path it hands back -- which here is the RAW Noether path, before
    # the tilt cap, the work-volume crop, EC_SCAN_REGION and the resample below.
    # On a cylindrical specimen that is 911 511 poses in and 180 539 out: four
    # fifths of the gate's work spent on poses about to be thrown away, and the
    # survivors never re-checked at the spacing they actually fly at. The gate
    # runs once, at the bottom of this function, on exactly the poses
    # run_ec_scan() will command.
    kwargs.setdefault("check_clearance", False)
    # AND THE REGION, so the mesh gate grades the surface being scanned rather
    # than the whole reconstruction. `region` is consumed here for the cost
    # estimate and the crop below, so it has to be handed on explicitly;
    # without it the gate measures the bench too, and a plan over a nearly flat
    # crown is refused because the flanks either side of it lean past 45
    # degrees.
    kwargs.setdefault("region", region)
    path, x3d = plan_mesh_path(mesh=mesh, line_spacing=line_spacing, **kwargs)
    reference = getattr(path, "roll_reference", NOETHER_ROLL_REFERENCE)
    roll_mode = getattr(path, "roll_mode", NOETHER_TOOL_ROLL)
    # AND THE SURFACE THE TOOL AXIS IS CONSTRAINED TO, for the same reason: every
    # crop below builds a fresh ScanPath, and _crop_path_by_mask() carries only the
    # source string. Held here so the orientation can be re-checked -- and
    # re-derived -- on the path that will actually fly rather than only on the raw
    # planner output. Without it the postcondition is verified before the tilt
    # crop, the preload, the reordering and the resample, and never after them.
    normal_surface = getattr(path, "normal_surface", None)
    normal_offset = getattr(path, "normal_offset", (0.0, 0.0, 0.0))
    normal_constrained = path_is_normal_constrained(path)
    uv = globals().get("last_uv_report") or {}
    if x3d is not None and uv.get("x3d") == x3d and uv.get("rejected"):
        # The projector would refuse this file when it is built at the scan's
        # first trace -- with the probe on the part -- and x3d_load_geometry can
        # abort the whole process doing it. Scan without it; the traces keep
        # their poses and project_traces() can paint them later.
        print("  WARNING: the projection surface %s would be REJECTED by the "
              "projector (MESHUV above). Continuing WITHOUT a projection surface: "
              "every trace is still recorded with its pose." % (x3d,))
        x3d = None
    # Tilt first, on the planned normals: a wall or a flank beyond the band is
    # dropped before the axis is averaged, so its normals never lean the passes
    # beside it. An average of axes inside the band stays inside it (a cone is
    # convex), so the smoothed path needs no second crop.
    if max_tilt is not None:
        # SAY WHAT THE CAP COSTS BEFORE TAKING IT. The drop below is the thing
        # that excludes: past the cable envelope's tilt these poses cannot be
        # flown, and removing them here -- before the axis is averaged -- is what
        # keeps a flank's normals from leaning the passes beside it. A bare count
        # of dropped waypoints says nothing about where on the part they were,
        # what they cost in travel, or whether the cause was bad normals rather
        # than an unreachable part. With sound normals the expected input loses
        # NOTHING here, so a nonzero count is a finding rather than routine.
        #
        # tool_reachability_report() measures and excludes nothing; the crop is
        # still the thing that excludes. Read its docstring for why this is NOT a
        # measure of normal error -- assert_probe_normal_to_surface() is.
        # Both names come from mesh_scan.dpi (included above), so either both are
        # here or neither. Fetched rather than called bare all the same: this is
        # REPORTING, and a reporting failure must never be able to stop a plan.
        _reach = globals().get("tool_reachability_report")
        _say_reach = globals().get("describe_tool_reachability")
        if _reach is not None and _say_reach is not None:
            try:
                for _line in _say_reach(_reach(path, max_tilt_deg=float(max_tilt))):
                    print(_line)
            except Exception as _e:
                print("  (no reachability report: %s: %s -- the crop below still "
                      "applies)" % (type(_e).__name__, _e))
        else:
            print("  (no reachability report: mesh_scan.dpi did not export it -- "
                  "the crop below still applies)")
        path = _crop_path_by_mask(
            path, tool_tilt_deg(path.quats) <= float(max_tilt),
            "eddy-current toolpath", min_points=2,
            why="tilted more than EC_MAX_TILT_DEG (%.0f deg)" % (float(max_tilt),))
    sm = None
    if smooth and roll_mode == "locked":
        path = smooth_tool_axis(path, smooth, reference=reference)
        # DECLINED IS A NORMAL OUTCOME, and it must not be an AttributeError.
        # smooth_tool_axis() returns the path UNTOUCHED -- with no
        # `axis_smoothing` -- when the path is already normal-constrained
        # (orient='normal'), because averaging the repaired normal is a tilt AWAY
        # from it, which is the one quantity that must be zero. It prints its own
        # reason when it declines. Reading the attribute unconditionally raises
        # at the very last line of the stage, after the toolpath is planned and
        # the X3D written -- a whole plan thrown away by a report.
        sm = getattr(path, "axis_smoothing", None)
        if sm is not None:
            print("  tool axis averaged over %.1f mm of travel "
                  "(EC_AXIS_SMOOTHING_M): moved up to %.1f deg, %.1f on average; "
                  "positions unchanged"
                  % (smooth*1e3, sm["moved_max_deg"], sm["moved_mean_deg"]))
    elif smooth:
        print("  NOT averaging the tool axis: the roll is %r, and averaging "
              "re-locks it" % (roll_mode,))
    # MINUS the preload along tool z, because tool z points AWAY from the part.
    # Every pose here has its tool axis on the outward surface normal -- that is
    # what orient="normal" builds and what tool_tilt_deg() measures against
    # straight up -- so -z is into the material and +z would be lift-off.
    # _offset_path_in_tool_frame() applies it in EACH POSE'S OWN FRAME, so on a
    # curved part the push follows the normal instead of going down in world z.
    #
    # And it happens HERE, before the work-volume and region crops below, so a
    # pose the preload pushes out of the work volume is cropped like any other.
    if preload:
        path = _offset_path_in_tool_frame(path, (0.0, 0.0, -float(preload)))
    print(describe_ec_preload(preload))
    path = _crop_path_to_work_volume(path, "eddy-current toolpath")
    if region:
        xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax = [float(v) for v in region]
        path = _crop_path_to_box(path, (xmin, ymin, -np.inf), (xmax, ymax, np.inf),
                                 "eddy-current toolpath", min_points=2,
                                 why="outside EC_SCAN_REGION")
    if EC_TRAVEL_DIRECTION is not None:
        path = orient_segments_along(path, EC_TRAVEL_DIRECTION,
                                     serpentine=EC_SERPENTINE)
        if EC_SERPENTINE:
            print("  raster order: the first pass travels along %s and the rest "
                  "alternate, so each starts where the last ended (%d reversed)"
                  % (tuple(EC_TRAVEL_DIRECTION), path.segments_reversed))
        else:
            print("  comb order: every pass travels along %s, returning to the "
                  "same side between passes (%d reversed)"
                  % (tuple(EC_TRAVEL_DIRECTION), path.segments_reversed))
    path.roll_reference = reference
    path.roll_mode = roll_mode
    if EC_WAYPOINT_SPACING_M and roll_mode == "locked":
        path = resample_segments(path, EC_WAYPOINT_SPACING_M, reference=reference)
        print("  passes resampled to %.2f mm between waypoints (%d -> %d waypoints)"
              % (EC_WAYPOINT_SPACING_M*1e3, path.resampled["waypoints_before"],
                 path.resampled["waypoints_after"]))
        path.roll_reference = reference
        path.roll_mode = roll_mode
    path.axis_smoothing = sm         # the crops above make new paths; keep it
    path.probe_rotation_deg = rotation
    path.normal_constrained = normal_constrained
    path.normal_surface = normal_surface
    path.normal_offset = normal_offset

    # THE ORIENTATION, RE-CHECKED ON THE PATH THAT WILL FLY. Everything above
    # rebuilds the path: the tilt crop and the region crop drop waypoints, the
    # preload moves every position along its own tool axis, the reordering
    # reverses passes and the resample puts waypoints somewhere new. The
    # postcondition inside plan_mesh_path() was measured before all of that.
    #
    # A turned-round pose is REPAIRED here, and one on surface facing away from
    # the probe is DROPPED with a count -- never turned over, which would point
    # the probe away from the part and drive the holder into it.
    if normal_surface is not None and normal_constrained:
        path, ec_axis_flip_report = repair_tool_axis_to_surface(
            path, normal_surface, what="eddy-current toolpath")
        path.probe_rotation_deg = rotation
        path.roll_reference = reference
        path.roll_mode = roll_mode
        path.axis_smoothing = sm
        path.normal_postcondition = assert_probe_normal_to_surface(
            path, normal_surface, what="eddy-current toolpath", mode="warn")

    # The tooling gate, on the poses that will actually fly. Contact is allowed
    # for the probe by name -- contact is what this scan IS -- and every other
    # link on the plate (the Keyence head 75 mm away, the Azure 230 mm away)
    # still has to clear. enforce_path_clearance() samples above
    # TOOLING_GATE_SAMPLE_ABOVE poses and certifies the rest; see
    # sampled_path_clearance() for what that guarantees.
    if EC_CHECK_CLEARANCE and _clearance_available("the eddy-current toolpath"):
        print("  contact is allowed for %s; every other link must clear the part"
              % (", ".join(TOOLING_CONTACT_LINKS) or "nothing",))
        # `mesh`, NOT `normal_surface`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
        #
        # normal_surface is the surface the TOOL AXIS is constrained to, and it is
        # cropped to the region being scanned. The clearance gate is asking a
        # different question: where may the rest of the tooling go. The Keyence
        # head is 75 mm to the side of the probe and the Azure 230 mm, so they
        # sweep well outside the scanned region -- over part that an ROI-cropped
        # mesh does not contain. Grading them against it would report clear
        # against specimen that is simply absent from the model. Measured on one
        # run's meshes: the region crop takes the part from 56 mm wide to 43 mm
        # and drops 3297 of 13450 faces.
        #
        # The sign is not a reason to prefer the repaired mesh either.
        # clean_mesh_for_planning() turns every face outward -- that is what its
        # rewinding pass is for -- and the signed distance takes its sign from
        # each face's own normal, so it does not need the mesh to be consistently
        # wound as a manifold. Verified on the real planning meshes: 0 faces
        # pointing down, and points 0.2 mm above the worst-wound faces all read
        # positive.
        _say_clearance_mesh(mesh, cr if EC_CLEAN_PLANNING_MESH else None)
        path, ec_clearance_report = enforce_path_clearance(
            path, mesh, allow_links=TOOLING_CONTACT_LINKS,
            what="eddy-current toolpath")
        twist_within_limit(path)
        path.roll_reference = reference
        path.roll_mode = roll_mode
        path.axis_smoothing = sm

    segs = path.acquiring_segments(min_length_m=PATH_MIN_SEGMENT_M)
    if not segs:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "no scanning pass is left on the eddy-current toolpath after "
            "cropping (passes under PATH_MIN_SEGMENT_M = %.0f mm are link moves). "
            "Check EC_SCAN_REGION against the part." % (PATH_MIN_SEGMENT_M*1e3,))
    path.roll_report = tool_roll_report(path.quats, segs, reference=reference)
    rr = path.roll_report
    print("  eddy-current toolpath: %d passes, %.3f m in contact"
          % (len(segs), sum(path.segment_length_m(s) for s in segs)))
    # The reference as a DIRECTION, whichever form it arrived in: the probe
    # rotation makes it a whole orientation, and four quaternion components after
    # "about x=" name the wrong thing.
    _ref_x = quat_to_matrix(np.asarray(roll_reference_quat(reference),
                                       dtype=np.float64))[:, 0]
    print("    roll %s about x=%s: %.2f deg across the path, %.2f within a pass; "
          "tilt up to %.1f deg (mean %.1f)"
          % (roll_mode, tuple(round(float(v), 6) for v in _ref_x),
             rr["twist_range_deg"],
             rr["twist_in_pass_deg"], rr["tilt_max_deg"], rr["tilt_mean_deg"]))
    # WHAT THIS PATH WAS PLANNED WITH, carried on the path itself. Every crop
    # above builds a new ScanPath, so this is set last, and run_ec_scan() reports
    # it rather than the current setting -- changing the preload does not move a
    # toolpath that is already planned.
    path.ec_preload_m = preload
    path.probe_rotation_deg = rotation
    say_path_probe_rotation(path)
    # AND THE CABLES, PER PASS, on the tilts this path really asks for. The check
    # at the top of this function is the untilted band, which every pose has to
    # pass; the band tightens as the tool leans, so a rotation can be holdable
    # over the crown of a part and not over its flanks. Refused here -- with the
    # pass named -- rather than when the scan reaches that pass with the probe on
    # the part. Nothing has moved yet.
    check_ec_probe_rotation(rotation, path=path)
    ec_scan_path, ec_scan_x3d = path, x3d
    return ec_scan_path, ec_scan_x3d


def _ec_hms(seconds):
    """h:mm:ss. Local on purpose: scan_parameters.dpi's _hms is private to that
    file, and a .dpi compiles as a function body, so borrowing it would work in
    the tests (one namespace) and NameError in a running config."""
    seconds = float(seconds)
    h, rem = divmod(int(round(seconds)), 3600)
    m, sec = divmod(rem, 60)
    return "%d:%02d:%02d" % (h, m, sec) if h else "%d:%02d" % (m, sec)


def _ec_bytes(n):
    """Bytes as MB/GB. Local, for the same reason as _ec_hms."""
    n = float(n)
    for unit, scale in (("GB", 1 << 30), ("MB", 1 << 20), ("kB", 1 << 10)):
        if n >= scale:
            return "%.1f %s" % (n/scale, unit)
    return "%d B" % (int(n),)


global ec_plan_cost
def ec_plan_cost(region=None, line_spacing=None, mesh=None, step_m=None,
                 velocity_mps=None, report=True):
    """What planning and scanning this area would cost -- BEFORE anything runs.

    The point of this function is that it needs no toolpath. Everything comes
    from the three technician controls (SCAN_LINE_SPACING between passes,
    SCAN_STEP_SIZE along one, SCAN_VELOCITY) and the extent of the area, so it
    can be printed while the operator is still choosing them, and plan_ec_path()
    can refuse before it commits the cell to half an hour of planning.

    The eddy-current stage is the one where the arithmetic bites. Halving the
    line spacing doubles the passes, and the passes are what everything scales
    on: at 0.30 mm a 200 x 150 mm area is ~500 passes, 100 m of travel, 100 000
    scanning waypoints -- and, because Noether plans at the mesh's own
    resolution and not at the scan's, 240 000 raw waypoints before the resample.

    Returns a dict; `errors` is empty only if the plan fits.
    """
    region = (EC_SCAN_REGION if region is None else region) or detected_region
    line_spacing = SCAN_LINE_SPACING if line_spacing is None else float(line_spacing)
    step_m = SCAN_STEP_SIZE if step_m is None else float(step_m)
    velocity_mps = SCAN_VELOCITY if velocity_mps is None else float(velocity_mps)
    mesh = (fine_mesh or rough_mesh) if mesh is None else mesh

    if region is not None:
        xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax = [float(v) for v in region]
        source = ("EC_SCAN_REGION" if region is EC_SCAN_REGION
                  else "detected" if region is detected_region
                  else "the region given")
    elif mesh:
        verts = _mesh_vertices(mesh)
        xmin, ymin = float(verts[:, 0].min()), float(verts[:, 1].min())
        xmax, ymax = float(verts[:, 0].max()), float(verts[:, 1].max())
        source = "the whole of %s" % (os.path.basename(str(mesh)),)
    else:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "nothing to price: pass region=(xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax), set "
            "EC_SCAN_REGION, or reconstruct a mesh first")
    width, height = abs(xmax - xmin), abs(ymax - ymin)

    # Which way the passes run. With NO rotation offset they run along the
    # SHORTER side of this rectangle and there is one every line_spacing along
    # the longer; pi/2 swaps that. noether_cut_axes() in noether_path.dpi is the
    # one statement of the rule, taken from the planner's source: the cutting
    # planes are normal to the mesh's SECOND principal axis at pi/2 and to the
    # FIRST at zero.
    #
    # AND THIS IS AN APPROXIMATION, for two reasons worth knowing before an
    # estimate is trusted. The planner takes its axes from a PCA of the MESH's
    # vertices, not from this rectangle: "longer side" is only "first principal
    # axis" when the part lies square to x and y, and the mesh it measures
    # includes whatever else the sensor saw. Pricing has to work before a mesh
    # exists, which is the whole point of this function, so it uses the
    # rectangle -- and mesh_plan_report() reports the real thing once there is
    # a mesh to measure.
    along, across = (min(width, height), max(width, height))
    if noether_pass_axis(globals().get("NOETHER_ROTATION_OFFSET", 0.0)) == 0:
        along, across = across, along
    passes = max(1, int(round(across/line_spacing)))
    travel = passes*along
    waypoints = passes*(int(along//EC_WAYPOINT_SPACING_M) + 1)
    raw_poses = int(travel/NOETHER_POSE_SPACING_M) if NOETHER_POSE_SPACING_M else 0

    reply_bytes = raw_poses*NOETHER_POSE_REPLY_BYTES
    # The path arrays themselves: 7 float64 per pose, and the crops hold the one
    # being built alongside the one being cropped.
    path_bytes = 2*raw_poses*7*8 + 2*waypoints*7*8
    # The clearance gate is now bounded rather than proportional to the path:
    # its distance queries run inside a byte budget and its poses in blocks, so
    # this is a constant. Taken from the module so there is one copy of it.
    gate_bytes = int(globals().get("SURFACE_QUERY_BYTES", 256 << 20))
    plan_bytes = reply_bytes + path_bytes + gate_bytes
    gate_seconds = waypoints*EC_GATE_SECONDS_PER_POSE
    plan_seconds = gate_seconds

    scan = build_scan_plan(travel, passes, step_m=step_m,
                           velocity_mps=velocity_mps, num_samples=1,
                           sample_bytes=16, force=True, geom_timeout=0.0)

    errors = []
    lim = EC_PLAN_LIMITS
    if raw_poses > lim["max_plan_poses"]:
        errors.append("the planner would return about %d waypoints, over the "
                      "%d this config will accept" % (raw_poses, lim["max_plan_poses"]))
    if plan_bytes > lim["max_plan_memory_bytes"]:
        errors.append("planning would need about %s of memory, over the %s limit"
                      % (_ec_bytes(plan_bytes), _ec_bytes(lim["max_plan_memory_bytes"])))
    if plan_seconds > lim["max_plan_seconds"]:
        errors.append("planning would take about %s, over the %s limit"
                      % (_ec_hms(plan_seconds), _ec_hms(lim["max_plan_seconds"])))

    out = {"region": (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax), "region_source": source,
           "width_m": width, "height_m": height, "line_spacing_m": line_spacing,
           "passes": passes, "travel_m": travel, "waypoints": waypoints,
           "raw_poses": raw_poses, "reply_bytes": reply_bytes,
           "path_bytes": path_bytes, "gate_bytes": gate_bytes,
           "plan_bytes": plan_bytes, "plan_seconds": plan_seconds,
           "gate_seconds": gate_seconds, "scan": scan,
           "errors": errors + list(scan["errors"]),
           "plan_errors": errors}
    if report:
        print("  what this plan costs, before it is built")
        print("    area               %.0f x %.0f mm  (%s)"
              % (width*1e3, height*1e3, source))
        print("    line spacing       %.2f mm  ->  %d passes, %.1f m of travel"
              % (line_spacing*1e3, passes, travel))
        print("    toolpath           %d waypoints at %.2f mm, from about %d the "
              "planner returns at the mesh's own %.2f mm"
              % (waypoints, EC_WAYPOINT_SPACING_M*1e3, raw_poses,
                 NOETHER_POSE_SPACING_M*1e3))
        print("    planning memory    %s  (%s the planner's reply, %s path arrays, "
              "%s the clearance gate)"
              % (_ec_bytes(plan_bytes), _ec_bytes(reply_bytes), _ec_bytes(path_bytes),
                 _ec_bytes(gate_bytes)))
        print("    planning time      %s  (the clearance gate, sampled)"
              % (_ec_hms(plan_seconds),))
        print("    then the scan      %s, %d traces, %s of trace data"
              % (_ec_hms(scan["scan_time_s"]), scan["traces"],
                 _ec_bytes(scan["memory_bytes"])))
        for e in errors:
            print("    PLANNING REFUSED: %s" % (e,))
        for e in scan["errors"]:
            print("    THE SCAN WILL BE REFUSED: %s" % (e,))
        for w in scan["warnings"]:
            print("    warning: %s" % (w,))
        if errors or scan["errors"]:
            print("    Scan a smaller area (EC_SCAN_REGION), widen "
                  "SCAN_LINE_SPACING, raise SCAN_STEP_SIZE -- or say so "
                  "deliberately: plan_ec_path(force=True) plans it anyway and "
                  "run_ec_scan(force=True) runs it.")
    return out


global preview_ec_scan
def preview_ec_scan(path=None):
    """What the scan would do, without moving. Print this before committing.

    The plan is derived from SCAN_STEP_SIZE and SCAN_VELOCITY: it says how many
    samples, how long it will take, how much memory it needs, and whether the
    requested velocity had to be scaled down to keep the pipeline fed. A plan
    over the limits is PRINTED here, with what is wrong; run_ec_scan() is what
    refuses it.

    The time is a floor. Its per-pass overhead (5 s) was measured on the UT
    scan, whose passes need no lift or descent; dry_run_ec_scan() measures the
    eddy-current one.
    """
    path = ec_scan_path if path is None else path
    if path is None:
        # NOTHING PLANNED YET, which is exactly when the operator most needs
        # this. Price the area from the three technician controls instead: it
        # takes no toolpath, no planner and no robot, and it is the answer to
        # "can I scan this at 0.30 mm?" before half an hour is spent finding
        # out. plan_ec_path() runs the same check and refuses on it.
        print("  no toolpath planned yet -- pricing the area from the current "
              "settings instead (plan_ec_path() will build it)")
        return ec_plan_cost()
    # min_length_m matches what the scan uses to decide which segments are
    # worth acquiring; without it the plan counts passes that will never run.
    segments = path.acquiring_segments(min_length_m=PATH_MIN_SEGMENT_M)
    length = sum(path.segment_length_m(s) for s in segments)
    # num_samples=1 and 16 bytes: an eddy-current reading IS one complex128.
    plan = plan_current_scan(length, len(segments),
                             step_m=SCAN_STEP_SIZE, velocity_mps=SCAN_VELOCITY,
                             num_samples=1, sample_bytes=16, force=True)
    plan["preload_m"] = say_path_preload(path)
    plan["probe_rotation_deg"] = say_path_probe_rotation(path)
    if plan["errors"]:
        print("  run_ec_scan() will REFUSE this plan as it stands. Scan a smaller "
              "area (EC_SCAN_REGION), widen SCAN_LINE_SPACING, or pass "
              "run_ec_scan(force=True) as a deliberate decision.")
    return plan


global ec_joint_report
def ec_joint_report(path=None, sample=20, per_pass=5):
    """wrist_3 along the planned toolpath from IK alone -- nothing moves.

    Solves `sample` passes spread across the path the way each is entered (far
    end first, then back along it, seeded). The range is what the cables see.
    """
    path = ec_scan_path if path is None else path
    if path is None:
        raise RuntimeError("no toolpath planned -- call plan_ec_path() first")
    allsegs = path.acquiring_segments(min_length_m=PATH_MIN_SEGMENT_M)
    ids = sorted(set(int(round(v)) for v in
                     np.linspace(0, len(allsegs) - 1, min(int(sample), len(allsegs)))))
    with motion_profile("scan"):
        r = path_joint_report(path, [allsegs[i] for i in ids], per_pass=per_pass)
    r["segment_ids"] = ids
    s = r["summary"]
    fmt = lambda v: "n/a" if v is None else "%.1f" % v
    print("  IK along %d of %d passes: %d solvable end to end; wrist_3 %s..%s deg, "
          "up to %s within one pass"
          % (len(ids), len(allsegs), s["ik_ok"], fmt(s["wrist3_min_deg"]),
             fmt(s["wrist3_max_deg"]), fmt(s["wrist3_in_pass_max_deg"])))
    # ...AND SAY WHETHER THE CABLES ALLOW IT, which nothing did at plan time.
    # plan_ec_path() calls twist_within_limit(), which measures Cartesian tool
    # twist against an unmeasured 90 degree placeholder and only warns. The
    # motion guard does refuse an out-of-band move, but not until the arm is
    # already scanning -- so the failure arrives as a run of dropped passes
    # mid-scan instead of a number before anything moved. EC_MAX_TILT_DEG = 45
    # lets waypoints through at tilts up to 44 degrees while the cable envelope
    # may only have been swept to +/-20; on one measured Keyence mesh 5.9% of
    # flying waypoints sat at tilts the envelope refuses outright.
    band = _ec_band_check(r)
    if band is not None:
        r["cable_band"] = band
    return r


global _ec_band_check
def _ec_band_check(report):
    """Compare a path_joint_report's wrist_3 against the measured cable band.

    Returns the finding, or None when this cell has no envelope (in which case
    the guard has already said wrist 3 is unchecked, and repeating it here adds
    nothing). Never raises: this is a report, and a planning stage that cannot
    ask the question must not fail because of it.
    """
    f = globals().get("wrist3_band_deg")
    if not callable(f):
        return None
    try:
        lo, hi = f(tilt=(0.0, 0.0))
    except Exception as e:                                        # noqa: BLE001
        print("  cable band: not checked (%s)" % (str(e).splitlines()[0][:70],))
        return None
    bad = []
    for k, entry in enumerate(report.get("passes", [])):
        a, b = entry.get("wrist3_min_deg"), entry.get("wrist3_max_deg")
        if a is None or b is None:
            continue
        if a < lo - 1e-6 or b > hi + 1e-6:
            bad.append((k, a, b))
    if not bad:
        print("  cable band: every solved pass is inside %.1f..%.1f deg "
              "(untilted band)" % (lo, hi))
        return {"lo_deg": lo, "hi_deg": hi, "outside": []}
    print("  CABLE BAND: %d of the solved passes need wrist_3 OUTSIDE "
          "%.1f..%.1f deg -- they will be refused when the scan reaches them, "
          "not now. Worst %.1f..%.1f deg."
          % (len(bad), lo, hi, min(x[1] for x in bad), max(x[2] for x in bad)))
    print("    Either sweep the cable envelope at the tilts this path needs, or "
          "lower EC_MAX_TILT_DEG (currently %s) until they are inside it."
          % (globals().get("EC_MAX_TILT_DEG"),))
    return {"lo_deg": lo, "hi_deg": hi, "outside": bad}


global run_ec_scan
def run_ec_scan(path=None, x3d=None, component=None, max_passes=None, force=False,
                save=True, run_dir=None):
    """Scan the planned toolpath in continuous motion.

    One complex impedance per SCAN_STEP_SIZE of travel, each carrying the pose it
    was taken at, conditioned and projected onto the surface.

    EVERY COMPLETED PASS IS WRITTEN TO DISK, one file per pass, into its own
    directory under SCAN_STORAGE_DIR beside the meshes -- so a scan that dies at
    pass 60 of 64 still leaves 59 passes and replay_ec_scan() can rebuild the
    image in a later session. The directory comes back in
    report["run_dir"]. save=False is for a bench run that is deliberately
    throwing its data away; see run_ec_path_scan().

    The acquisition is shared with the ultrasound scan (surface_path_scan.dpi);
    what is eddy-current here is what a reading is and which conditioned scalar
    gets painted onto the part. `component` picks that: "vertical" once the
    rotation is set -- lift-off is horizontal by then and what is left is the
    part -- or "magnitude", which ignores the rotation and is the safe choice
    before one has been chosen.

    The probe is in contact: every pass is entered by lift / transit / descend
    along the tool axis, swept at no more than NEAR_PART_SPEED, and the probe is
    lifted clear at the end, error or not. The warning light is on throughout.
    How far past the measured surface the tip is commanded is the path's own
    contact preload -- set_ec_preload() -- and this prints it.

    The INSTRUMENT is nulled once, at the first touchdown, before any trace is
    read: an eddy-current bridge has to be balanced on the part. set_ec_nulling()
    turns that off or makes a failure non-fatal, and the outcome comes back in
    report["instrument_null"]. Not the same as the conditioning null
    (/ec/cond/null, null_here()), which is a software offset on top of it.

    A pass that will not plan is dropped and reported. The traces taken are
    still correct, because each carries its own pose; the coverage is not.
    """
    path = ec_scan_path if path is None else path
    x3d = ec_scan_x3d if x3d is None else x3d
    if path is None:
        raise RuntimeError("no toolpath planned -- call plan_ec_path() first")
    # ec_cond is NOT required: the conditioning is wired by the scan itself,
    # once the first reading has given it a trace list to read. Requiring it
    # here would make the scan impossible to start.
    if ec_source is None:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "eddy current is not set up -- call setup_ec() first (and, once "
            "there are traces, null_here() and set_rotation() before trusting "
            "what it paints)")
    _check_contact_speed()
    # WHAT THE TIP IS COMMANDED ONTO, from the path rather than from the current
    # setting: a plan built before the preload was last changed still carries the
    # older push.
    say_path_preload(path)
    # AND WHICH WAY THE COIL IS TURNED, for the same reason: a split-D answers to
    # cracks perpendicular to its split, so this is what the scan is looking for,
    # and a plan built before set_probe_rotation() was last called still carries
    # the older rotation.
    say_path_probe_rotation(path)
    # AND WHAT WILL HAPPEN ABOUT THE INSTRUMENT NULL, before the arm moves. An
    # absent instrument is knowable now, so a required null refuses here instead
    # of a minute in with the probe on the part. A null command that fails is not
    # knowable until it is issued, and that is caught at the first touchdown.
    print(describe_ec_nulling())
    if (EC_NULL_ON_CONTACT and EC_NULL_REQUIRED
            and ec_instrument is None and ec_model is None):
        raise RuntimeError(
            "there is no eddy-current instrument to null on the part "
            "(EC_INSTRUMENT=%r) and a null is required, so this refuses before "
            "the arm moves. An instrument balanced in air has its operating "
            "point in the wrong place and every trace of the scan rides at an "
            "offset that no software null repairs. connect_ec_instrument() opens "
            "one; set_ec_nulling(required=False) accepts an un-nulled scan as a "
            "decision; set_ec_nulling(enabled=False) is for bench work."
            % (EC_INSTRUMENT,))
    with warning_light("eddy-current scan"):
        report = run_ec_path_scan(path, x3d, ec_source, ec_cond,
                                  prefix="/ec", staging_path="/ec/current",
                                  cond_prefix=EC_COND_PREFIX,
                                  component=component, max_passes=max_passes,
                                  force=force, pitch_m=SCAN_STEP_SIZE,
                                  contact=_ec_contact(),
                                  null_instrument=(null_ec_instrument
                                                   if EC_NULL_ON_CONTACT else None),
                                  null_required=EC_NULL_REQUIRED,
                                  save=save, run_dir=run_dir)

    # CONDITION THE IMAGE BEFORE ANYONE LOOKS AT IT.
    #
    # The projection paints /ec/cond/vertical, and with the rotation unset that
    # is the raw imaginary part: the picture is the instrument's standing offset
    # and the tilt, with the flaws a few per cent of it. The angle that fixes it
    # is measurable from the scan's own traces and from nothing else -- it
    # belongs to this probe at this frequency on this instrument today -- so the
    # scan measures it rather than leaving an operator to find it by eye on an
    # image that does not show the flaws yet.
    #
    # AFTER the scan, not during: the projector paints each reading as it
    # arrives, so a rotation applied halfway leaves the first half painted with
    # the old one. This sets it and re-paints every trace.
    #
    # NEVER FATAL. The scan is the measurement and it is already on disk; this
    # is presentation, and a scan that refused to return because it could not
    # pick an angle would be the tail wagging the dog. A failure says so and
    # leaves the unrotated image, which is what the previous behaviour was.
    if EC_AUTO_ROTATION:
        try:
            degrees, detail = rotation_from_scan()
            report["rotation_measured_deg"] = degrees
            report["rotation_detail"] = detail
        except Exception as _e:                            # noqa: BLE001
            report["rotation_measured_deg"] = None
            report["rotation_error"] = "%s: %s" % (type(_e).__name__, _e)
            print("  could not measure the lift-off rotation (%s: %s)"
                  % (type(_e).__name__, _e))
            print("    the traces are unaffected and on disk; the IMAGE is "
                  "unrotated, so set_rotation() and project_ec_traces() by hand, "
                  "or replay_ec_scan(rotation_deg=...).")
    return report


global ec_dry_run_report
ec_dry_run_report = None

global dry_run_ec_scan
def dry_run_ec_scan(path=None, passes=None, sample=5, park=False):
    """Fly the eddy-current toolpath exactly as run_ec_scan() would, measuring
    instead of acquiring. THE FIRST THING TO RUN ON THE PROBE'S BEHALF.

    Same approach (lift / transit / descend), same sweep speed, same motion
    profile, same warning light -- no DAQ, no trigger, nothing staged. By
    default `sample` passes spread across the path; passes=[...] picks them
    (indices into the scanning passes), sample=None flies them all.

    Prints and returns what was flown against what was planned: passes entered
    and swept, the tip's distance from the planned line and along the normal,
    tilt and how well it followed the normal, roll against the locked reference,
    the peak speed of every kind of motion, the closest the tip came to the
    part between passes, and the wrist_3 range. Left in ec_dry_run_report.

    In simulation there is no spring and no contact, so "along the normal" is
    tracking error only. On the cell it is what the fixture had to absorb.
    """
    global ec_dry_run_report
    path = ec_scan_path if path is None else path
    if path is None:
        raise RuntimeError("no toolpath planned -- call plan_ec_path() first")
    _check_contact_speed()
    allsegs = path.acquiring_segments(min_length_m=PATH_MIN_SEGMENT_M)
    if not allsegs:
        raise RuntimeError("no scanning passes on this toolpath")
    if passes is not None:
        ids = [int(i) for i in passes]
    elif sample and int(sample) < len(allsegs):
        ids = sorted(set(int(round(v)) for v in
                         np.linspace(0, len(allsegs) - 1, int(sample))))
    else:
        ids = list(range(len(allsegs)))
    segs = [allsegs[i] for i in ids]
    length = sum(path.segment_length_m(s) for s in segs)
    # The plan is what sets the sweep speed (raster_velocity()); force=True
    # because a dry run of a few passes of an over-long scan is the point.
    plan_current_scan(length, len(segs), step_m=SCAN_STEP_SIZE,
                      velocity_mps=SCAN_VELOCITY, num_samples=1, sample_bytes=16,
                      force=True)
    print("EC dry run: passes %s of %d -- the scan's own motion, nothing acquired"
          % (ids, len(allsegs)))
    with warning_light("eddy-current dry run"):
        try:
            ec_dry_run_report = fly_surface_path(
                path, segs=segs, contact=_ec_contact(), label="EC dry run",
                reference=getattr(path, "roll_reference", NOETHER_ROLL_REFERENCE),
                segment_ids=ids)
        finally:
            if park:
                park_arm()
    return ec_dry_run_report


global ec_touch_down
def ec_touch_down(index=0, path=None):
    """Put the probe on the part at the start of pass `index`, and stop there.

    For nulling the instrument on good material with the probe in contact. It
    arrives the way every pass does: lift, transit in the air, descend along the
    tool axis at NEAR_PART_SPEED. ec_lift() takes it off again.
    """
    path = ec_scan_path if path is None else path
    if path is None:
        raise RuntimeError("no toolpath planned -- call plan_ec_path() first")
    segs = path.acquiring_segments(min_length_m=PATH_MIN_SEGMENT_M)
    start, end = segs[int(index)]
    with warning_light("eddy-current touch-down"):
        with motion_profile("scan"):
            ok = contact_approach(_ec_contact(), path.positions, path.quats,
                                  start, end - 1)
    if not ok:
        raise RuntimeError("could not put the probe on the start of pass %d" % (index,))
    print("  probe on the part at %s (start of pass %d). Null the instrument "
          "now; ec_lift() when done." % (np.round(path.positions[start], 4), index))
    return tuple(float(v) for v in path.positions[start])


global ec_lift
def ec_lift():
    """Lift the probe EC_APPROACH_M clear of the part, along its own axis."""
    with warning_light("eddy-current lift"):
        return contact_retract(_ec_contact(),
                               positions=None if ec_scan_path is None
                               else ec_scan_path.positions)


# ==============================================================================
# THE AZURE GAUGE: the rough mesh must agree with the Keyence before a raster
# is planned over it
# ==============================================================================
# Stage 3 plans the Keyence raster OVER the stage 2 Azure mesh, so every
# millimetre that mesh reads LOW is a millimetre the profiler really flies
# CLOSER to the part. A mesh reading 13.6 mm low across a surface-ground plate
# (22 mm at one end) flies the head at 66 mm and then at about 58 mm against its
# 57 mm hard limit: 26-39% of a 10 inch plate comes back as garbage and the
# probe comes close to touching the sample.
#
# verify_calibration() measures the same disagreement standalone, and has read
# -11.6 to -13.6 mm on this cell. These two functions are what stops
# run_automated_scan() flying a raster over a mesh that far out.
#
# REFUSE-ONLY: a passing gauge does NOT shift the mesh. A pass means the
# disagreement is inside AZURE_GAUGE_MAX_MM (2.0 mm), which is under 9% of the
# head's +/-23 mm window -- so a correction applied here would buy margin that
# was never binding, while making this config a SECOND place where a z
# correction is applied. The Azure's depth bias belongs in exactly one place,
# the manifest's depth adapter, where the TSDF, verify_calibration() and RViz
# all see the same surface (see azure-depth-reads-12mm-long). Shifting the file
# here would also make the gate the author of the surface it had just graded:
# the next run's gauge would be measuring a mesh this code had already moved,
# with nothing on disk saying so.
#
# NEITHER IS DECLARED global, deliberately. They are private to this config and
# are reached only from run_automated_scan() below -- the .dgp body is their
# enclosing scope, exactly as _ec_hms() and _ec_bytes() are reached from
# ec_plan_cost() -- and azure_gauge.dpi is included AFTER this point, so a name
# exported from here could be silently replaced by one of that module's.

def _ec_gauge_verdict(res, max_mm=None, ref_dist_m=None, near_limit_m=None,
                      allow=False, skip_why=None):
    """What the Azure gauge's answer means for the raster about to be planned.

    PURE: no ROS, no motion, no printing, no globals, so every branch is
    covered on the host by azure_gauge_gate_test.py.

    `res` is gauge_mesh_against_keyence()'s dict. res["dz_mm"] is POSITIVE when
    the MESH READS LOW, which is the direction that costs standoff. Returns
    (verdict, lines):

        "skip"      nothing was gauged, and nothing is claimed -- carry on
        "pass"      the mesh agrees with the Keyence inside max_mm
        "refuse"    it does not, and stage 3 must not run
        "override"  it does not, and allow= plans over it anyway

    `lines` is what to print, or what to raise with when the verdict is
    "refuse".
    """
    # NOTHING TO DECIDE. A clean no-op that SAYS SO: silence here would be
    # indistinguishable from a gauge that passed.
    if skip_why:
        return ("skip", ["  the rough mesh was NOT gauged against the Keyence: "
                         "%s. Nothing in this run checked the standoff stage 3 "
                         "will fly at; verify_calibration() is the standalone "
                         "check." % (skip_why,)])
    if not isinstance(res, dict):
        return ("refuse", ["stage 3 is refused: the Azure gauge returned %r "
                           "rather than a result dict, so the mesh is "
                           "ungauged." % (res,)])
    if max_mm is None:
        return ("refuse", ["stage 3 is refused: the gauge ran but "
                           "AZURE_GAUGE_MAX_MM is not defined, so there is no "
                           "tolerance to gate on."])

    dz = res.get("dz_mm")
    # NaN IS NOT None, AND THAT LEAKED FABRICATED ARITHMETIC INTO A REFUSAL.
    # When nothing was measured, gauge_mesh_against_keyence() reports dz_mm as
    # NaN rather than None -- the mean of no samples. Every guard below tested
    # `dz is not None`, which NaN passes, so a refusal that measured 0 of 3
    # points still printed "would fly at nan mm, which is nan mm INSIDE the
    # head's 57 mm hard limit". A number nobody measured must not appear in the
    # sentence that tells an operator how close the head will fly. Treat
    # non-finite as absent, here, once, so every branch downstream inherits it.
    if dz is not None:
        dz = float(dz)
        if dz != dz or dz == float("inf") or dz == float("-inf"):
            dz = None
    n_meas, n_req = int(res.get("n_measured") or 0), int(res.get("n_requested") or 0)
    resid = res.get("residual_mm")
    over = "" if resid is None else ", residual %.2f mm" % (float(resid),)

    # WHAT IT COSTS, in the units the failure arrives in. The raster is planned
    # at the reference distance above the MESH, so a mesh that reads dz low puts
    # the head dz closer to the real surface. Same arithmetic
    # verify_calibration() prints, with dz's sign the other way round.
    consequence = ""
    if dz is not None and ref_dist_m is not None and near_limit_m is not None:
        flown = float(ref_dist_m)*1e3 - dz
        margin = flown - float(near_limit_m)*1e3
        consequence = ("A raster planned over it at the %.0f mm reference "
                       "distance would fly at %.1f mm, which is %.1f mm %s the "
                       "head's %.0f mm hard limit."
                       % (float(ref_dist_m)*1e3, flown, abs(margin),
                          "clear of" if margin >= 0.0 else "INSIDE",
                          float(near_limit_m)*1e3))

    if bool(res.get("ok")) and dz is not None and abs(dz) <= float(max_mm):
        return ("pass", ["  the rough mesh agrees with the Keyence to %+.2f mm "
                         "over %d of %d points%s (tolerance %.1f mm; + means "
                         "the mesh reads low). %s"
                         % (dz, n_meas, n_req, over, float(max_mm),
                            consequence)])

    if not bool(res.get("ok")) or dz is None:
        lines = ["the Azure gauge could not measure the rough mesh against the "
                 "Keyence -- %d of %d points: %s"
                 % (n_meas, n_req, res.get("why") or "no reason given")]
    else:
        lines = ["the Azure rough mesh reads %.2f mm %s against the Keyence "
                 "over %d of %d points%s, and the tolerance is %.1f mm."
                 % (abs(dz), "LOW" if dz >= 0.0 else "HIGH", n_meas, n_req,
                    over, float(max_mm))]
    if consequence:
        lines.append(consequence)
    lines.append("A 13.6 mm error of this kind has flown the head at 66 mm, then "
                 "at 58 mm against the 57 mm limit, and 26-39% of a 10 inch "
                 "plate came back as garbage.")
    if allow:
        return ("override",
                ["  ** PLANNING THE KEYENCE RASTER OVER THIS MESH ANYWAY "
                 "(allow_azure_bias):"] + ["     " + l for l in lines])
    return ("refuse", ["stage 3 is refused: " + lines[0]]
            + ["  " + l for l in lines[1:]]
            + ["  Fix the Azure's depth bias and re-run -- verify_calibration() "
               "measures it and the correction belongs in the manifest's depth "
               "adapter. allow_azure_bias=True plans the raster over it "
               "anyway."])


def _ec_azure_gauge(mesh, allow=False):
    """Fly the Keyence over the rough mesh and decide whether stage 3 may run.

    Raises ValueError on a refusal, which run_automated_scan()'s `finally`
    turns into a parked arm. Returns {"verdict", "result", "why"} either way,
    so capture_full_pipeline() records what was measured.

    NO GAUGE IN globals() WARNS AND CONTINUES, and does not refuse. The include
    at the bottom of this file is unconditional, so in a normal session the only
    way to get here is a module that loaded without exporting its entry point --
    but the same contract tooling_clearance.dpi and keyence_standoff.dpi already
    have with the stages that use them applies: an absent optional module is a
    deployment state, not a measurement. A gauge that IS present and cannot
    measure -- no profiles, too few points, an exception -- is a measurement,
    and that refuses.
    """
    say = globals().get("_cal_say") or print
    gauge = globals().get("gauge_mesh_against_keyence")
    res, skip = None, None
    if gauge is None:
        skip = "azure_gauge.dpi is not loaded, so this session has no gauge"
    elif not mesh:
        skip = ("this run has no rough mesh -- stage 2 produced nothing, or a "
                "caller skipped it")
    else:
        try:
            _n = globals().get("AZURE_GAUGE_POINTS")
            res = gauge(mesh=mesh, say=say, **({} if _n is None else {"n": _n}))
        except Exception as e:                                    # noqa: BLE001
            # NOT re-raised as itself: a gauge that dies has told us nothing
            # about the mesh, which is a refusal with a reason, not a traceback
            # out of the middle of a five stage run.
            res = {"ok": False, "n_measured": 0, "n_requested": 0, "dz_mm": None,
                   "tilt_mrad": None, "residual_mm": None, "samples": [],
                   "why": "the gauge raised %s: %s" % (type(e).__name__, e)}
    verdict, lines = _ec_gauge_verdict(
        res, max_mm=globals().get("AZURE_GAUGE_MAX_MM"),
        ref_dist_m=globals().get("KEYENCE_REF_DIST_M"),
        near_limit_m=(globals().get("KEYENCE_RANGE_M") or (None,))[0],
        allow=bool(allow), skip_why=skip)
    if verdict == "refuse":
        raise ValueError("\n".join(lines))
    for line in lines:
        say(line)
    return {"verdict": verdict, "result": res, "why": skip}


global run_automated_scan
def run_automated_scan(index=0, scan=True, cutoff=None, line_spacing=None,
                       region=None, park=True, release_yolo_after_detection=True,
                       roi_select=None, allow_azure_bias=False):
    """Detect, measure, plan -- and scan, if asked.

    The whole point of the exercise: one call, no questions. It prints what it
    is doing at each stage and leaves every intermediate result in a global, so
    a stage that fails can be retried on its own without redoing the ones before
    it.

    scan=False stops after the plan, which is the useful mode while the cell is
    being set up: it flies the reconstruction sweeps and shows what the scan
    would be without committing to it. dry_run_ec_scan() is the next step.

    region=(xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax) skips detection and measures there -- for a
    part the detector cannot see.

    roi_select=False runs with no operator: the detected region goes straight
    to the Keyence raster, which is what this did before the selection window
    existed. None follows the ROI_SELECT config flag (on), True forces the
    window on for one run. Note that the region is picked AFTER the Azure
    sweep, so roi_select=False does not make the run any shorter up to that
    point -- it only stops it waiting for a human.

    allow_azure_bias=True plans the Keyence raster over the Azure rough mesh
    even when the gauge says the two disagree by more than AZURE_GAUGE_MAX_MM.
    EC_ALLOW_AZURE_BIAS in a config is the same override for an unattended run.
    Default False, and it is False for a reason -- see stage 2a.

    The work volume is checked FIRST, before anything moves. The warning light
    is held for the whole run, and park=True rises out of the work volume and
    goes to PARK_POSE at the end, error or not.
    """
    global detected_region
    require_work_volume("the automated scan")
    out = {}
    _detected = False
    # SUPERVISED, so stopping External Control on the pendant is a PAUSE.
    # Unsupervised, stopping the robot program mid-sweep deactivates the
    # controllers, MoveIt sits on the in-flight goal until its duration monitor
    # fires, and every later row is "planned in full, EXECUTION refused" against
    # a controller answering "Can't accept new action goals" -- the sweep
    # finishes and saves a mesh with holes in it. Inside this block that becomes
    # a hold at the next row boundary, and ScanAborted if it is not resumed.
    with supervised("the automated scan"), warning_light("automated scan"):
        try:
            # BEFORE ANYTHING IS ASKED OF THE PLANNER. A contact scan that did not
            # reach its own lift-off -- an exception, a killed process -- leaves
            # the probe resting on the specimen, and the planner then refuses
            # every goal because it validates the START state first. The refusal
            # lands on the next run's first move, which is a move to a named pose
            # that has nothing to do with the part, and says nothing about the
            # part. This backs the probe off along its own axis, which is the one
            # motion available from there; anything else in collision is reported
            # and left alone. Costs one service call when there is nothing wrong.
            out["start_state"] = recover_contact_start_state(
                contact=_ec_contact(),
                positions=(ec_scan_path.positions
                           if ec_scan_path is not None else None))

            if region is None:
                print("1/5  detecting the specimen")
                region = detect_part(index=index)
                _detected = True
            else:
                region = tuple(float(v) for v in region)
                detected_region = region
                print("1/5  region given by hand, no detection: x %.3f..%.3f  "
                      "y %.3f..%.3f" % (region[0], region[2], region[1], region[3]))
            out["region"] = region

            # DETECTION IS OVER. Everything from here -- two reconstruction
            # sweeps, a mesh filter, a Noether plan, the clearance gate -- is
            # the expensive half, and it is where a run gets OOM-killed.
            # yolo_node has been measured at 646% of one core at the cell and
            # still holds 400% after detection has finished, with its model
            # loaded and its image subscription live. Release it here, once,
            # whichever branch above ran: a region given by hand never touches
            # the detector at all, so it never turns it off either, which is how
            # a run with no detection still pays for one.
            if release_yolo_after_detection:
                release_yolo(quiet=True)

            # GIVE MOVEIT SOMETHING TO AVOID BEFORE THE SWEEP, not after it.
            # Without this call the planning scene holds no world objects during
            # the Azure sweep, tari_cpp_mtc logs "Collision checking this
            # Cartesian path against a planning scene with NO world objects",
            # and every collision check that stage makes is vacuous. The box is
            # replaced by the real mesh as soon as the sweep produces one (same
            # object id), so it is only the scaffolding for the stage that has
            # no mesh.
            #
            # NEVER FATAL. A box is a guess -- the detected top, over the
            # support the work volume declares -- and a wrong guess must not be
            # able to stop a scan that ran perfectly well without one before.
            try:
                _support = float(require_work_volume("the specimen box")[1][2])
                # Only this call's detection, never a stale one: a region
                # given by hand does not touch detected_info, and using the
                # previous part's surface z would put the box at the wrong
                # height without saying so.
                _top = (float(detected_info.get("surface_z"))
                        if (_detected and detected_info
                            and detected_info.get("surface_z") is not None)
                        else None)
                if _top is None:
                    print("  no detected surface z for this region, so no "
                          "specimen box: MoveIt will collision-check the sweep "
                          "against an empty world")
                elif _top - _support < 1e-3:
                    print("  the detected top (%.3f) is not above the support "
                          "(%.3f), so no specimen box" % (_top, _support))
                else:
                    specimen_box_to_planning_scene(
                        region=region, support_z=_support, height=_top - _support)
            except Exception as _e:
                print("  WARNING: no specimen box in the planning scene (%s). "
                      "The sweep is collision-checked against the robot only."
                      % (_e,))

            print("2/5  Azure sweep (rough planning surface, purpose='planning')")
            out["rough"] = reconstruct_rough(region=region)

            # DOES THE MESH AGREE WITH THE KEYENCE? Stage 3 plans the raster
            # over what stage 2 just made, so this is the seam: after the rough
            # mesh exists and before anything is planned on top of it. See
            # _ec_azure_gauge() above for what it refuses and for why a pass
            # does not shift the mesh.
            #
            # BEFORE 2b, NOT AFTER IT. Nothing 2b does changes the surface the
            # gauge grades, so putting the gate later would only mean asking a
            # human to draw a region on a picture of the part 12 mm out of place
            # for a run that is then refused anyway. The whole reason the ROI
            # window sits where it does is not to spend the operator on a scan
            # that is not going to happen; a failing gauge is that case.
            print("2a/5 gauging the rough mesh against the Keyence")
            out["gauge"] = _ec_azure_gauge(
                out.get("rough"),
                allow=bool(allow_azure_bias
                           or globals().get("EC_ALLOW_AZURE_BIAS", False)))

            # THE OPERATOR NARROWS THE REGION, HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE.
            # After stage 2 because this is the first 3D surface that exists
            # and it cost 40 seconds; before stage 3 because that is the
            # 10-minute Keyence raster and the whole point is not to spend it
            # on the bench. The detector's region is preselected, so the
            # operator corrects a box rather than drawing one, and Accept
            # without touching anything gives back what stage 1 said.
            #
            # Unavailable (no display, no PyQt5) prints why and carries on with
            # the detected region: this must not be able to stop an unattended
            # run that worked before the window existed. CANCEL IS DIFFERENT --
            # that is a deliberate "no" from somebody who is standing there,
            # and it stops the scan.
            # AND TIGHTEN THE REGION TO WHERE THE PART ACTUALLY IS, before the
            # operator is shown it and before the 10-minute raster is planned
            # over it. yolo_region() projects a BOUNDING BOX, and a box round a
            # part is bigger than the part: a 200 x 200 mm plate has been
            # detected as 200 x 217 mm. That 8% is bench, no scan can ever cover
            # it, and region_coverage -- which wants 90% -- then refuses a
            # Keyence measurement that covered 98% of the plate. The gate is
            # right about the wrong question.
            #
            # The rough mesh is the first thing that knows where the part is, so
            # this is the first moment the question can be answered. It only ever
            # SHRINKS the box; the operator's own selection below still wins.
            _tighten = globals().get("region_of_measured_surface")
            if region is not None and out.get("rough") and callable(_tighten):
                try:
                    region = _tighten(out["rough"], region)
                    out["region"] = region
                except Exception as _e:                           # noqa: BLE001
                    print("    (could not tighten the region to the measured "
                          "surface: %s: %s)" % (type(_e).__name__, _e))

            out["roi"] = None
            if roi_select is not False:
                print("2b/5 region of interest -- draw what should be scanned")
                # line_spacing reaches the window because it is what decides
                # whether a drawn region is wide enough to scan at all, and this
                # run's spacing is the one that matters -- not the config's,
                # when the caller overrode it.
                region, out["roi"] = refine_region_interactively(
                    region, out["rough"], enabled=roi_select,
                    title="Region to scan -- Azure rough mesh",
                    line_spacing=line_spacing)
                out["region"] = region

            print("3/5  Keyence raster (the measurement)")
            out["fine"] = reconstruct_fine(rough=out["rough"], region=region)

            print("4/5  filtering the mesh")
            out["mesh"] = filter_mesh(out["fine"], cutoff=cutoff)

            print("5/5  planning the eddy-current toolpath")
            # THE REGION REACHES THIS STAGE TOO. Stage 1 said where the part is;
            # without passing it on, Noether plans over every triangle of the
            # measured mesh -- which is a reconstruction of whatever the sensor
            # saw, table and neighbouring specimens included. For a
            # 301 x 156 mm part that has come out as 18 394 passes, 579 m of
            # travel and 588 614 traces at 1 mm pitch. That is not a slow plan,
            # it is the wrong part. EC_SCAN_REGION still wins when somebody set
            # it, because that is the deliberate "scan this corner" control.
            # THE SELECTION REACHES THIS STAGE TOO, and for a different reason
            # than the region does. The region crops the toolpath; the selection
            # tells the mesh gate WHICH SURFACE its roughness and tilt numbers
            # are about. Without it the gate grades the optical breadboard, the
            # clamps and the mount block along with the part, and it loses
            # almost all of its discrimination: over a whole mesh a bad scan and
            # a good one have read 55.5 and 52.6 deg normal-turn p99, 6% apart,
            # while over the plate alone the same two read 10.5 and 4.5 -- 2.3x.
            out["path"], out["x3d"] = plan_ec_path(
                mesh=out["mesh"], line_spacing=line_spacing,
                region=EC_SCAN_REGION if EC_SCAN_REGION else region,
                roi=out["roi"])
            out["plan"] = preview_ec_scan(out["path"])

            if not scan:
                print("stopping before the scan (scan=False). dry_run_ec_scan() "
                      "flies it without the instrument; run_ec_scan() when ready.")
                return out

            print("scanning")
            # ec_source, not ec_cond: the conditioning is wired by the scan and
            # is None right up to the first staged trace, so testing ec_cond
            # here would call setup_ec() on every run. The thing being set up is
            # the SOURCE.
            if ec_source is None:
                setup_ec()
            out["report"] = run_ec_scan(out["path"], out["x3d"])
            return out
        finally:
            if park and PARK_POSE:
                try:
                    park_arm()
                except Exception as e:
                    print("  WARNING: could not park the arm (%s)" % (e,))


include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "robot_supervisor.dpi"))
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "cell_capture.dpi"))

# Sensor-to-robot calibration. AFTER cell_capture.dpi, which owns the motion
# gate, the scenario recorder and the taught points it uses.
#
# The routine call is verify_calibration(): two minutes, no artifact and no
# touch-off, and it returns how far the Azure is from the Keyence over a flat.
# At 13.6 mm the Keyence raster, planned over the Azure's mesh, flies the head
# at 58 mm against its 57 mm limit and 26-39% of a 10 inch plate comes back as
# garbage. Run it before a session; calibrate_azure() is for when it fails.
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "calibration.dpi"))
try:
    print("")
    print(describe_calibration())
except Exception as _e:
    print("  calibration: could not read the record (%s)" % (_e,))

# THE AZURE GAUGE, which is verify_calibration()'s measurement turned into a gate
# over the mesh a run is actually about to use. run_automated_scan()'s stage 2a
# calls it; _ec_azure_gauge() warns and carries on if this file is absent.
#
# LAST, AND IT HAS TO BE. It reuses calibration.dpi's Keyence motion and profile
# helpers (_cal_geom, _cal_profile_points, _cal_say), so it must load AFTER that
# file -- which is itself loaded after cell_capture.dpi for the motion gate --
# and after keyence_standoff.dpi, which owns KEYENCE_RANGE_M and
# KEYENCE_REF_DIST_M, the window the gauge's numbers are reported against.
# The order here IS the load order: a .dpi is a function body sharing one
# globals(), so a file that reads a name an earlier include has not defined yet
# gets a NameError at load, and one that redefines a name silently wins.
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "azure_gauge.dpi"))

# THE SIMULATION HAS NOTHING TO SCAN WITHOUT THIS.
# The Gazebo world is deliberately bare -- `ros2 service call /get_model_list`
# returns ['ur'] and nothing else, not even a ground plane, because the stock
# empty.world made gzserver stall for minutes downloading sun/ground_plane. The
# table and the specimen exist as MoveIt COLLISION GEOMETRY and in the URDF, so
# planning sees them and the SENSORS DO NOT: the Azure images empty space and the
# simulated Keyence reports "0.0% of columns returned" forever. Every stage from
# reconstruct_rough() onward then works on nothing, without failing.
#
# place_gazebo_specimen() puts the test plate into the running world so there IS
# a surface. UTPlaneScan.dgp includes it for the same reason. Without it the
# pipeline cannot be exercised end to end in simulation.
#
# Included unconditionally because it only DEFINES functions and starts nothing:
# its Gazebo service calls happen when it is called, and on hardware nobody calls
# it. Needs write_plate_ply() from noether_path.dpi and the TEST_PLATE_* /
# SCAN_ORIGIN constants above, so it must come after both.
include(None, os.path.join(SCAN_MODULE_DIR, "sim_specimen.dpi"))


global place_ec_test_plate
def place_ec_test_plate(plate_dir=None):
    """Put the simulated eddy-current test plate into the running world.

    ONE CALL, because the plate has to reach THREE places that do not talk to
    each other, and a plate in two of them is worse than a plate in none:

        the Gazebo model      what the simulated camera renders
        the Keyence scene     what the simulated profiler raycasts against
        ec_model              where the flaws are, for the instrument

    The first two are place_gazebo_mesh()'s job and it does both. The third is
    already loaded -- the model was built from EC_PLATE at startup -- so all
    this adds is the CHECK that the two agree: the same directory, and the
    manifest the model is using sitting beside the mesh being spawned. Spawn a
    plate from one directory while the instrument reads flaws from another and
    every notch is in the wrong place, with nothing anywhere saying so, and the
    scan comes out looking like a projection fault.

    `plate_dir` defaults to EC_PLATE's own directory, which is the arrangement
    tools/make_ec_test_plate.py produces: mesh and manifest together.
    """
    if plate_dir is None:
        if not EC_PLATE:
            raise RuntimeError(
                "EC_PLATE is not set, so there is no test plate to place. "
                "Generate one with tools/make_ec_test_plate.py and start with "
                "EC_PLATE=<dir>/flaws.json.")
        plate_dir = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(EC_PLATE)))
    stl = os.path.join(plate_dir, "ec_test_plate.stl")
    if not os.path.isfile(stl):
        raise RuntimeError(
            "no %s. place_ec_test_plate() spawns the mesh that "
            "tools/make_ec_test_plate.py writes beside flaws.json; point "
            "plate_dir at that directory." % (stl,))
    if ec_model is not None:
        mine = os.path.abspath(ec_model.plate.get("_path", ""))
        if os.path.dirname(mine) != os.path.abspath(plate_dir):
            raise RuntimeError(
                "the instrument is reading flaws from %s but you are spawning "
                "the mesh from %s. One of them is the wrong plate, and a scan "
                "run like this puts every notch in the wrong place with "
                "nothing to show it. Pass the matching directory, or restart "
                "with EC_PLATE pointing at this one." % (mine, plate_dir))
    out = place_gazebo_mesh(stl, name="ec_test_plate")
    if ec_model is None:
        print("    NOTE: the plate is in the world but the instrument has no "
              "model of it (EC_TABLE/EC_PLATE unset), so the readings are "
              "still the synthetic signal. The geometry is real; the numbers "
              "are not.")
    else:
        print("    the instrument's %d notches are on this plate, scanned face "
              "at z = %.4f m" % (len(ec_model.flaws), ec_model.surface_z))
    return out


# ==============================================================================
# THE CABLE GUARD, ON FOR THE WHOLE SESSION
# ==============================================================================
# LAST, after every module is loaded and after `ros` exists, and OUTSIDE any
# capture scenario.
#
# The capture tool gates wrist 3 only inside capture_scenario(..., pipeline=True),
# so a session started normally and driven with run_automated_scan() would
# otherwise check nothing: an unguarded sweep has walked wrist_3 from +29
# degrees through zero to -113, against a measured tight point of 31.3 degrees
# at that tilt. Installing the guard here refuses every one of those moves.
#
# install_cable_guard() wraps `ros`, and pipeline code looks `ros` up as a
# global at call time, so nothing downstream changes. A refusal comes back as a
# failed move, not an exception, so a sweep loses a row instead of abandoning a
# running TSDF. cable_guard_off() lifts it for a block;
# capture_cable_envelope() is the one caller that needs to.
try:
    install_cable_guard()
except Exception as _e:
    print("  WARNING: THE CABLE GUARD IS NOT INSTALLED (%s). Wrist 3 is "
          "unchecked; do not run an automated scan until this is fixed." % (_e,))

# ...AND THE SECOND HALF OF IT, for the callers the wrapper does not cover.
# install_cable_guard() wraps the `ros` NAME, so anything that resolves `ros` as
# a global at call time is checked. ApproachInConfiguration picks the IK branch
# for a whole pass INSIDE the motion module, before any of that, and
# surface_path_scan.dpi, mesh_scan.dpi and the sweep's own _enter() reach it
# directly. Handing the band to the module lets it reseed an out-of-band
# solution at the band centre instead of returning one the guard will then
# refuse. None is its default and means "behave as before".
try:
    ros.WRIST3_BAND = wrist3_band()
    print("  wrist 3 band handed to the motion module: %.1f..%.1f deg"
          % (np.degrees(ros.WRIST3_BAND[0]), np.degrees(ros.WRIST3_BAND[1])))
except KeyError:
    # Nothing measured for this cell. wrist3_band() says so by raising; the
    # guard has already warned, and IK keeps its old behaviour.
    pass
except Exception as _e:
    print("  WARNING: could not hand the wrist 3 band to the motion module "
          "(%s); IK will not reseed out-of-band solutions." % (_e,))

print("ECAutomatedScan ready.")
# WHAT IS ACTUALLY LOADED, with the path each component came from. The cell, the
# container and a developer's host run different builds of this stack, and the
# differences do not announce themselves: an install behind the workspace shows up
# as a fault that was fixed long ago, and a compiled extension older than the
# library it links against shows up as a segfault. Neither is diagnosable from a
# log that does not say what was imported.
try:
    print(describe_software_versions())
except Exception as _e:                                       # noqa: BLE001
    print("  software versions: unavailable (%s)" % (_e,))
print("  step %.3f mm along a pass, %.2f mm between passes, sweep %.1f mm/s"
      % (SCAN_STEP_SIZE*1e3, SCAN_LINE_SPACING*1e3, SCAN_VELOCITY*1e3))
print("  near the part %.0f mm/s, away %.0f mm/s, joint moves at MoveIt scaling %s; "
      "warning light on digital output %s"
      % (NEAR_PART_SPEED*1e3, TRANSIT_SPEED*1e3, JOINT_MOVE_SCALING, WARNING_LIGHT_PIN))
_ok, _why = ec_ready()
print("  eddy current: %s" % (_why,))
try:
    print("  " + describe_work_volume())
except Exception as _e:
    print("  work volume: unavailable (%s)" % (_e,))
# AND THE SAFE MOTION VOLUME, because what it permits is the whole reason a
# tilted sensor pose can be flown at all. Silent when the cell declares none,
# which is the honest state for a room nobody has walked -- and that silence
# means "the motion volume bounds navigation, as it always did".
try:
    _safe = describe_safe_motion_volume()
    if _safe:
        print("  " + _safe.strip().replace("\n", "\n  "))
except Exception as _e:                                       # noqa: BLE001
    print("  safe motion volume: unavailable (%s)" % (_e,))
# HOW THE SENSORS ARE POINTED, and the one call that changes it. Both default to
# the behaviour that has always been flown here, so an unchanged run is
# unchanged; the line exists because a sweep flown normal to the part and a
# sweep flown straight down produce the same log lines everywhere else.
try:
    print(describe_sensor_orientation())
except Exception as _e:                                       # noqa: BLE001
    print("  sensor orientation: unavailable (%s)" % (_e,))
# WHETHER TOOL SPEED IS COMMANDED IN M/S OR CONVERTED THROUGH THE CURVE. Off by
# default -- the curve is what has always been used -- but the two produce
# identical log lines everywhere else, and the curve over-predicted by up to 38%
# on the last cell run, so which one is in force has to be stated once at the
# top rather than inferred from a scaling later.
try:
    print(describe_tool_speed_solver())
except Exception as _e:                                       # noqa: BLE001
    print("  tool speed: unavailable (%s)" % (_e,))
try:
    print("  " + describe_cable_envelope().replace("\n", "\n  "))
except Exception as _e:
    print("  cable envelope: unavailable (%s)" % (_e,))
# SAY NOW whether stage 2a will check the Azure against the Keyence, and whether
# a failure will stop the run. Same reason as the region-of-interest line below:
# an operator who finds out at stage 3 that nothing was gauged has already spent
# the sweep, and one who finds out that the gate is overridden has already lost
# the plate.
try:
    if globals().get("gauge_mesh_against_keyence") is None:
        print("  Azure gauge: NOT AVAILABLE (azure_gauge.dpi did not load). "
              "Run verify_calibration() before committing to a raster.")
    elif EC_ALLOW_AZURE_BIAS:
        print("  Azure gauge: measured at stage 2a but NOT ENFORCED "
              "(EC_ALLOW_AZURE_BIAS is True). A biased mesh will be rastered "
              "over, which is what costs a plate.")
    else:
        print("  Azure gauge: stage 2a gauges the rough mesh against the "
              "Keyence over %s points and refuses the raster past %.1f mm "
              "(allow_azure_bias=True to override)"
              % (globals().get("AZURE_GAUGE_POINTS", "?"),
                 float(globals().get("AZURE_GAUGE_MAX_MM", float("nan")))))
except Exception as _e:
    print("  Azure gauge: could not be described (%s)" % (_e,))
# SAY NOW whether the operator will be asked to draw the region, and if not,
# why not. Finding out that PyQt5 is missing 40 seconds into a run, with the
# arm parked over the part, is the failure this line exists to prevent.
try:
    if not ROI_SELECT:
        print("  region of interest: OFF (ROI_SELECT); the detected region "
              "goes straight to the Keyence raster")
    else:
        _ok, _why = roi_select_available()
        print("  region of interest: %s"
              % ("the operator draws it after the Azure sweep -- %s" % (_why,)
                 if _ok else "UNAVAILABLE, so the detected region is used "
                             "as-is: %s" % (_why,)))
except Exception as _e:
    print("  region of interest: could not be checked (%s)" % (_e,))
print("")
# check_rough_plan(), NOT check_sweep_plan(). The first IS
# reconstruct_rough(plan_only=True) -- the same source, the same kwargs, the
# same planner -- so it cannot describe a different sweep from the one that
# flies. The second is the PAN sweep's pre-flight, and this stage defaults to
# a dome, so it grades a sweep that is never flown.
print("  before you commit: check_rough_plan()  -- plans stage 2 without moving")
print("           anything, through the same planner it will fly, and says")
print("           whether it reaches, at what standoff and with what headroom.")
print("  stages:  detect_part()  reconstruct_rough()  gauge_mesh_against_keyence()")
print("           select_roi(mesh)  reconstruct_fine()  filter_mesh()")
print("           plan_ec_path()  preview_ec_scan()")
print("  motion:  dry_run_ec_scan()  ec_joint_report()  ec_touch_down()  ec_lift()")
print("           park_arm()")
print("  setup:   setup_ec()  null_here()  set_rotation(deg)  set_gain(h, v)")
# NAMED ONLY IN SIMULATION, because on hardware there is nothing to place and a
# prompt offering it invites somebody to try.
if EC_SOURCE == "sim":
    print("  sim:     place_ec_test_plate()  -- puts the test plate into the "
          "running")
    print("           world, for the camera, the profiler and the instrument at "
          "once.")
    if ec_model is None:
        print("           The instrument is on its SYNTHETIC signal: set "
              "EC_TABLE and")
        print("           EC_PLATE to drive it from a generated response table "
              "instead.")
    else:
        print("           ec_model.report() says what the instrument is "
              "modelling%s"
              % (" -- A STUB TABLE, INVENTED PHYSICS"
                 if ec_model.table.is_stub else ""))
# THE DATA OUTLIVES THE PROCESS, and the operator has to know where. Named on
# the prompt because the moment these are wanted is after a crash, when reading
# source is the last thing anybody wants to be doing.
print("  on disk: every completed pass is written to %s/ec_scan_<time>/"
      % (SCAN_STORAGE_DIR,))
print("           ec_save_status() says how much has reached it; list_ec_runs()")
print("           lists saved runs; replay_ec_scan() rebuilds the image from one")
print("           -- replay_ec_scan(rotation_deg=40) tries a different rotation")
print("           over the SAME traces, which is why the raw impedance is kept.")
# set_probe_rotation() TURNS THE ARM; set_rotation() turns a phase. Two very
# different verbs one letter apart, so they are named on separate lines with what
# each one moves.
print("  probe:   set_probe_rotation(deg) turns the COIL about its own axis -- a")
print("           split-D answers to cracks across its split, so this is which")
print("           flaw direction is being looked for. set_ec_preload(m) is how")
print("           hard it presses. set_rotation(deg) is the instrument's phase")
print("           and moves nothing.")
print("  sensors: set_sensor_orientation(azure=, keyence=) -- 'z' locks the tool")
print("           axis to world z, 'normal' follows the specimen's surface, and")
print("           'orbit' (Azure only) flies the part from several sides. It")
print("           takes effect at the next reconstruct_rough/reconstruct_fine;")
print("           sensor_orientation() reads it without changing anything.")
print("  all of it: run_automated_scan(scan=False)   region=(xmin,ymin,xmax,ymax) "
      "skips detection")
print("             roi_select=False runs it with nobody there")
print("             allow_azure_bias=True rasters over a mesh the gauge failed")
print("  first run on hardware: ROS-Scanning/docs/ec-automated-scan-first-run.md")
